window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-bio-microbes","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Microbes in Human Welfare · Practice","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about methanogens are correct?\n(i) Methanobacterium is a methanogen.\n(ii) Methanogens are commonly found in the anaerobic sludge formed during sewage treatment.\n(iii) Methanogens release oxygen as a by-product of methane formation.\n(iv) In the rumen, methanogens help in the breakdown of cellulose.\n(v) Methanogens require a continuous supply of air to grow.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT names Methanobacterium as a common methanogen.\n(ii) Correct: methanogens are commonly found in anaerobic sludge.\n(iii) Incorrect: their products are CH4, CO2 and H2, not oxygen.\n(iv) Correct: they aid cellulose breakdown in the rumen.\n(v) Incorrect: they grow anaerobically.\nThat makes three correct statements.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a benefit that NCERT lists for plants having mycorrhizal associations?","image":"","options":["Resistance to root-borne pathogens","Tolerance to salinity and drought","Fixation of atmospheric nitrogen by the fungus for the plant","Overall increase in plant growth and development"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists these benefits of mycorrhiza:\n(1) Listed: resistance to root-borne pathogens.\n(2) Listed: tolerance to salinity and drought.\n(4) Listed: overall increase in plant growth and development.\n(3) Not listed: the fungal partner (e.g., Glomus) supplies phosphorus, not fixed atmospheric nitrogen. Nitrogen fixation is done by bacteria such as Rhizobium and by cyanobacteria.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Bacillus thuringiensis is sold as dried spores that are mixed with water and sprayed onto plants such as brassicas and fruit trees.\n(ii) The Bt toxin is released in the gut of insect larvae after they eat the sprayed plant parts.\n(iii) Bt spray kills caterpillars as well as all other insects that visit the sprayed plants.\n(iv) Baculoviruses are broad-spectrum insecticides that also harm non-target insects.\n(v) Baculoviruses are especially desirable in integrated pest management (IPM) programmes and in ecologically sensitive areas.\n(vi) Bt-cotton contains Bt toxin genes introduced by genetic engineering.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Bt is available in sachets as dried spores, which are mixed with water and sprayed.\n(ii) Correct: the toxin is released in the larval gut and kills the larvae.\n(iii) Incorrect: Bt kills caterpillars but leaves other insects unharmed.\n(iv) Incorrect: baculoviruses are species-specific and narrow-spectrum, with no harm to non-target insects.\n(v) Correct: this specificity makes them suited to IPM and ecologically sensitive areas.\n(vi) Correct: Bt toxin genes were transferred into cotton by genetic engineering.\nCorrect statements: (i), (ii), (v), (vi), which makes four.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about BOD are correct?\n(i) It is the amount of oxygen that would be consumed if all the organic matter in one litre of water were oxidised by bacteria.\n(ii) The BOD test measures the rate of oxygen uptake by micro-organisms in a water sample.\n(iii) Waste water with a higher BOD has a lower polluting potential.\n(iv) BOD is an indirect measure of the organic matter present in water.\n(v) Sewage is kept in the aeration tank until its BOD rises to a maximum.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the NCERT definition of BOD.\n(ii) Correct: the test measures how fast micro-organisms take up oxygen.\n(iii) Incorrect: the greater the BOD, the greater the polluting potential.\n(iv) Correct: oxygen demand reflects the amount of organic matter present.\n(v) Incorrect: sewage is treated until its BOD is reduced, not raised.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"In the secondary treatment of sewage, 'flocs' are best described as:","image":"","options":["Masses of bacteria associated with fungal filaments forming mesh-like structures","Clumps of soil and small pebbles that settle in the primary tank","Floating debris removed by sequential filtration","Colonies of anaerobic methanogens found in sludge digesters"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In aeration tanks, useful aerobic microbes grow vigorously into flocs.\nNCERT defines flocs as masses of bacteria associated with fungal filaments that form mesh-like structures.\nSoil and pebbles are grit, which is removed during primary treatment, so option (2) is wrong.\nMethanogens act later, in the anaerobic sludge digesters, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of municipal sewage treatment in the correct sequence:\nP. Anaerobic bacteria digest bacteria and fungi in the sludge, producing methane, H$_2$S and CO$_2$\nQ. Floating debris is removed by sequential filtration\nR. Air is pumped into the effluent, aerobic microbes grow into flocs and BOD falls\nS. Grit settles by sedimentation, forming primary sludge\nT. Bacterial flocs settle in a settling tank, forming activated sludge","image":"","options":["Q → S → R → T → P","S → Q → R → P → T","Q → S → T → R → P","Q → R → S → T → P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Primary (physical) treatment comes first:\n- Floating debris is removed by filtration (Q).\n- Grit settles as primary sludge (S).\nSecondary treatment follows:\n- The primary effluent is aerated, flocs form and BOD drops (R).\n- The flocs then settle in a settling tank as activated sludge (T).\nFinally, most of this sludge goes to anaerobic digesters, where biogas is produced (P).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"In a typical gobar gas plant, the floating cover placed over the dung slurry:","image":"","options":["keeps sinking as the slurry is digested","keeps rising as gas is produced by microbial activity","is fixed and acts as the outlet for spent slurry","is opened regularly to let air in for the microbes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The cover floats over the slurry in the concrete tank.\nAs microbes produce gas, the gas collects under the cover and pushes it upward, so the cover keeps rising.\nSpent slurry leaves through a separate outlet, not through the cover.\nAdmitting air would be harmful because the methanogens are anaerobic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (product) with List-II (microbe producing it):\nList-I\nA. Streptokinase\nB. Cyclosporin A\nC. Statins\nD. Citric acid\nList-II\n(i) Monascus purpureus\n(ii) Aspergillus niger\n(iii) Streptococcus\n(iv) Trichoderma polysporum","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Streptokinase is made by the bacterium Streptococcus, so A-(iii).\nB. Cyclosporin A is made by the fungus Trichoderma polysporum, so B-(iv).\nC. Statins are made by the yeast Monascus purpureus, so C-(i).\nD. Citric acid is made by the fungus Aspergillus niger, so D-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Methanobacterium produces large amounts of methane when it grows:","image":"","options":["aerobically on starchy material","aerobically on cellulosic material","anaerobically on cellulosic material","anaerobically on fats and oils"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that certain bacteria growing anaerobically on cellulosic material produce large amounts of methane along with CO2 and H2.\nThese bacteria are called methanogens, and Methanobacterium is a common example.\nThe aerobic options are wrong because methanogens grow without oxygen.\nStarch and fats are not the substrate described for them.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Cyclosporin A, an immunosuppressive agent used in organ-transplant patients, is produced by Trichoderma polysporum","Bottled fruit juices are clarified using pectinases and lipases","Statins from Monascus purpureus lower blood cholesterol by competitively inhibiting an enzyme of cholesterol synthesis","Streptokinase from Streptococcus, modified by genetic engineering, is used as a clot buster"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: cyclosporin A comes from the fungus Trichoderma polysporum.\n(2) Incorrect: bottled juices are clarified by pectinases and proteases. Lipases are used in detergents to remove oily stains.\n(3) Correct: statins act as competitive inhibitors of an enzyme in cholesterol synthesis.\n(4) Correct: genetically modified streptokinase is used to remove clots in heart-attack patients.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Fleming not only discovered penicillin but also himself established its full potential as an effective antibiotic before World War II.\nStatement II: Penicillin is a chemical made by a fungus that can kill or retard the growth of bacteria such as Staphylococci.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Fleming discovered penicillin, but Ernest Chain and Howard Florey established its full potential much later.\nStatement II is correct. Penicillin is produced by the mould Penicillium notatum and stopped Staphylococci from growing on Fleming's plate.\nSo only Statement II is correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about biogas production are correct?\n(i) A biogas plant has a concrete tank about 10-15 feet deep into which dung slurry is fed.\n(ii) The floating cover over the slurry remains fixed at one level throughout gas production.\n(iii) The spent slurry removed from the plant may be used as a fertiliser.\n(iv) Methanogens growing anaerobically on cellulosic material produce methane along with $CO_2$ and $H_2$.\n(v) The main gas produced during fermentation of dough and in cheese making is methane.\n(vi) Methanogens such as Methanobacterium are commonly found in the anaerobic sludge during sewage treatment.","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the plant uses a concrete tank 10-15 feet deep.\n(ii) Incorrect: the floating cover keeps rising as gas accumulates in the tank.\n(iii) Correct: the spent slurry is removed through a separate outlet and can be used as fertiliser.\n(iv) Correct: methanogens produce $CH_4$ along with $CO_2$ and $H_2$.\n(v) Incorrect: the main gas in dough, cheese and beverage fermentation is $CO_2$.\n(vi) Correct: methanogens are present in anaerobic sludge.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Wine\n(B) Whisky\n(C) Fermentor\n(D) Brewer's yeast\nList-II:\n(I) Saccharomyces cerevisiae\n(II) Fermented broth that is distilled\n(III) Very large vessel for growing microbes industrially\n(IV) Fermented product that is not distilled","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A-IV: Wine is made without distillation.\nB-II: Whisky is made by distilling the fermented broth.\nC-III: Fermentors are the very large vessels used for industrial-scale growth of microbes.\nD-I: Brewer's yeast is Saccharomyces cerevisiae.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"When lactic acid bacteria convert milk into curd, the nutritional quality of the product improves. This is mainly because of an increase in which vitamin?","image":"","options":["Vitamin $B_{12}$","Vitamin C","Vitamin D","Vitamin K"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that converting milk to curd improves its nutritional quality by increasing vitamin $B_{12}$.\nLactic acid bacteria (LAB) such as Lactobacillus multiply in the milk and bring about this change.\nVitamins C, D and K are not linked to curd formation in the chapter.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Aspergillus niger, a bacterium, is used for the commercial production of citric acid.\nStatement II: Acetobacter aceti, a bacterium, is used for the commercial production of acetic acid.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Aspergillus niger does produce citric acid, but it is a fungus, not a bacterium.\nStatement II is correct. Acetobacter aceti is a bacterium, and it produces acetic acid.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about biological control is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Bacillus thuringiensis spores sprayed on plants are eaten by caterpillars; the toxin released in the larval gut kills them but leaves other insects unharmed","Most baculoviruses used as biocontrol agents belong to the genus Nucleopolyhedrovirus and are species-specific, narrow-spectrum insecticides","The Ladybird beetle is used to get rid of mosquitoes, and dragonflies are used to control aphids","Trichoderma species are free-living fungi common in root ecosystems and are effective biocontrol agents of several plant pathogens"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: Bt toxin is released in the larval gut and kills butterfly caterpillars selectively.\n(2) Correct: Nucleopolyhedrovirus species are narrow-spectrum and harmless to non-target organisms. This makes them suitable for integrated pest management (IPM).\n(3) Incorrect: the pairing is reversed. Ladybirds control aphids, and dragonflies control mosquitoes.\n(4) Correct: Trichoderma is a fungal biocontrol agent of plant pathogens.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"A brewery makes two drinks with Saccharomyces cerevisiae.\nDrink X: malted cereal is fermented and the broth is NOT distilled.\nDrink Y: fruit juice is fermented and the broth is then distilled.\nX and Y are most likely:","image":"","options":["X – whisky; Y – wine","X – beer; Y – wine","X – whisky; Y – rum","X – beer; Y – brandy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"X is made from malted cereal and is not distilled. Of the drinks offered, only beer fits.\nWhisky is ruled out for X because it is distilled.\nY is a distilled drink made from fruit juice, which is brandy.\nWine is ruled out for Y because it is undistilled.\nRum is ruled out for Y because it is not made from fruit juice; it comes from sugarcane products such as molasses.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"In a mycorrhizal association formed by members of the genus Glomus, the fungal partner mainly absorbs which nutrient from the soil and passes it to the plant?","image":"","options":["Nitrogen","Potassium","Phosphorus","Magnesium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Mycorrhiza is a symbiotic association between a fungus and plant roots.\nNCERT states that the fungal symbiont (e.g., Glomus) absorbs phosphorus from the soil and passes it to the plant.\nNitrogen is the tempting wrong option. In this chapter, nitrogen fixation is linked to bacteria and cyanobacteria, not to Glomus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Primary sludge\n(B) Primary effluent\n(C) Activated sludge\n(D) Anaerobic sludge digester\nList-II\n(i) Sediment of bacterial flocs in the settling tank after aeration\n(ii) Supernatant of the primary settling tank, sent for secondary treatment\n(iii) All the solids that settle during primary treatment\n(iv) Tank where bacteria digest the bacteria and fungi of the sludge, releasing biogas\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): the solids settling in primary treatment form the primary sludge.\nB-(ii): the supernatant of the primary settling tank is the primary effluent, which goes to the aeration tanks.\nC-(i): flocs that sediment after BOD reduction form the activated sludge.\nD-(iv): anaerobic bacteria in the digesters digest the sludge microbes and produce methane, H$_2$S and CO$_2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The majority of baculoviruses used as biological control agents belong to the genus:","image":"","options":["Trichoderma","Nucleopolyhedrovirus","Bacillus","Methanobacterium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Baculoviruses are pathogens that attack insects and other arthropods.\nAccording to NCERT, most baculoviruses used in biocontrol belong to the genus Nucleopolyhedrovirus.\nTrichoderma is a fungus and Bacillus is a bacterium, so neither is a viral genus.\nMethanobacterium is a methanogen involved in biogas production.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The puffed-up appearance of the fermented dough used for making idli and dosa is due to","image":"","options":["methane released by methanogens in the dough","oxygen released during photosynthesis by microbes","hydrogen sulphide produced by anaerobic bacteria","carbon dioxide produced during bacterial fermentation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Idli and dosa dough is fermented by bacteria.\nFermentation releases $CO_2$ gas, which gets trapped in the dough and makes it puff up.\nMethane is produced by methanogens in biogas plants and sludge digesters, not in dough.\nMicrobes in dough do not photosynthesise, and $H_2S$ is a sludge-digester gas.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about sewage treatment is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In primary treatment, floating debris is removed by sequential filtration and grit is removed by sedimentation","In aeration tanks, aerobic microbes grow into flocs and consume the major part of the organic matter, lowering the BOD","The major part of the activated sludge is pumped back into the aeration tank to act as inoculum","In anaerobic sludge digesters, bacteria digest the bacteria and fungi of the sludge, producing methane, hydrogen sulphide and carbon dioxide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Primary treatment is a physical process of filtration followed by sedimentation.\n(2) Correct. Flocs are bacteria associated with fungal filaments, and they reduce the BOD.\n(3) Incorrect. Only a small part of the activated sludge is returned as inoculum. The major part goes to the anaerobic sludge digesters.\n(4) Correct. The gases produced there form biogas.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following organisms can fix atmospheric nitrogen and are therefore useful as nitrogen-enriching biofertilisers?\nRhizobium, Azospirillum, Azotobacter, Glomus, Anabaena, Nostoc, Trichoderma, Oscillatoria","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Nitrogen fixers in this list:\nRhizobium fixes nitrogen in root nodules of legumes (symbiotic).\nAzospirillum and Azotobacter fix nitrogen while free-living in soil.\nAnabaena, Nostoc and Oscillatoria are nitrogen-fixing cyanobacteria.\nNot nitrogen fixers:\nGlomus is a mycorrhizal fungus that supplies phosphorus to the plant.\nTrichoderma is a fungus used as a biocontrol agent.\nTotal nitrogen fixers = 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The large holes in Swiss cheese and the gas collected in a gobar gas plant are both due mainly to methane produced by microbes.\nStatement II: The type of gas a microbe produces depends on the microbe and on the organic substrate it utilises.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. The holes in Swiss cheese come from large amounts of CO2 made by Propionibacterium sharmanii.\nMethane is the main gas only in biogas, where it is made by methanogens growing anaerobically on cellulose.\nStatement II is correct: NCERT says the gas produced depends on the microbes and the substrates they use.\nIn dough, cheese and beverages the main gas is CO2, whereas methanogens produce mostly CH4.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Bacterial colonies growing in a petri dish can be seen with the naked eye.\nReason (R): Individual bacterial cells are large enough to be seen with the naked eye.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. On nutritive media, bacteria multiply into colonies containing huge numbers of cells, and these colonies are visible to the naked eye.\nR is false. Single bacterial cells are microscopic. NCERT shows them magnified about 1500X in its figure.\nThe colony is visible only because of the mass of cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following drinks are obtained by distillation of a fermented broth?\nWine, Whisky, Beer, Rum, Brandy, Toddy","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Wine: not distilled.\nWhisky: distilled.\nBeer: not distilled.\nRum: distilled.\nBrandy: distilled.\nToddy: made simply by fermenting palm sap, with no distillation.\nSo 3 of the drinks are distilled.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Wine and beer are produced without distillation, whereas whisky, brandy and rum are obtained by distillation of the fermented broth.\nStatement II: The yeast used for fermenting malted cereals and fruit juices to make these beverages is the same species used to ferment bread dough.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that wine and beer are made without distillation. Whisky, brandy and rum are made by distilling the fermented broth.\nStatement II is correct. Brewer's yeast is Saccharomyces cerevisiae, which is also baker's yeast.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which microbes, when grown on nutritive media, form colonies that can be seen with the naked eye, as described in NCERT?","image":"","options":["Viruses and viroids","Bacteria and many fungi","Prions and viroids","Only protozoa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that bacteria and many fungi can be grown on nutritive media to form colonies visible to the naked eye. Figure 8.3 shows bacterial and fungal colonies in petri dishes.\nViruses, viroids and prions cannot grow on nutritive media alone, because they need living host cells.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about microbes is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Viroids are infectious agents that are smaller than viruses and lack a protein coat","Prions are infectious particles consisting of nucleic acid enclosed in a protein coat","A bacteriophage is a virus whose host is a bacterium","Some microbes can survive in highly acidic environments"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Viroids are free RNA molecules without a protein coat, and they are smaller than viruses.\n(2) Incorrect. Prions are proteinaceous infectious agents. Nucleic acid inside a protein coat describes a virus.\n(3) Correct. Bacteriophages infect bacteria.\n(4) Correct. NCERT lists highly acidic environments among the habitats of microbes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about traditional fermented foods and cheese is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Microbes are used to ferment fish, soyabean and bamboo-shoots to make foods","Propionibacterium sharmanii, which forms holes in Swiss cheese, is a bacterium","Both the holes of Swiss cheese and the flavour of Roquefort cheese result from fungal activity","Toddy is a traditional drink obtained by fermenting the sap of palms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT lists fish, soyabean and bamboo-shoots as foods made by microbial fermentation.\n(2) Correct: Propionibacterium sharmanii is a bacterium.\n(3) Incorrect: the holes in Swiss cheese come from a bacterium. Only Roquefort ripening involves a fungus.\n(4) Correct: toddy is fermented palm sap.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The dough used for making bread is fermented using","image":"","options":["Saccharomyces cerevisiae","Lactobacillus","Penicillium notatum","Monascus purpureus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT: bread dough is fermented using baker's yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae.\nLactobacillus is a lactic acid bacterium used for curd.\nPenicillium notatum is the source of penicillin.\nMonascus purpureus produces statins.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"During digestion in anaerobic sludge digesters, bacteria produce a mixture of gases that forms biogas. This mixture mainly consists of:","image":"","options":["Methane, hydrogen sulphide and carbon dioxide","Oxygen, nitrogen and carbon dioxide","Methane, ammonia and oxygen","Ethylene, carbon monoxide and hydrogen sulphide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In anaerobic sludge digesters, anaerobic bacteria digest the bacteria and fungi present in the sludge.\nThis digestion releases methane, hydrogen sulphide and CO$_2$. Together these gases form inflammable biogas.\nOxygen cannot be a product here because the process is anaerobic, which rules out options (2) and (3).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Biogas, used as fuel in rural areas, contains which gas as its predominant component?","image":"","options":["Carbon dioxide","Methane","Hydrogen sulphide","Hydrogen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines biogas as a mixture of gases produced by microbial activity that contains predominantly methane.\nCO2 and H2 are also produced, but in smaller amounts than methane.\nH2S appears in sludge-digester gas but is only a minor part.\nCO2 is the tempting choice because it is the main gas in dough and beverage fermentation, not in biogas.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (biocontrol agent) with List-II (target).\nList-I:\n(A) Ladybird\n(B) Dragonfly\n(C) Bacillus thuringiensis\n(D) Trichoderma\nList-II:\n(i) Mosquitoes\n(ii) Plant pathogens\n(iii) Aphids\n(iv) Butterfly caterpillars","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-i, B-iii, C-iv, D-ii","A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-iv, B-i, C-iii, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Ladybird feeds on aphids, so A-iii.\n(B) Dragonflies are used against mosquitoes, so B-i.\n(C) Bacillus thuringiensis kills butterfly caterpillars, so C-iv.\n(D) Trichoderma, a fungus of root ecosystems, acts against plant pathogens, so D-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which option correctly compares how Swiss cheese and Roquefort cheese get their characteristic features?","image":"","options":["Swiss cheese: large holes due to CO$_2$ from Propionibacterium sharmanii; Roquefort cheese: ripened by growing a specific fungus","Swiss cheese: ripened by a specific fungus; Roquefort cheese: large holes due to CO$_2$ from Propionibacterium sharmanii","Swiss cheese: large holes due to methane from Methanobacterium; Roquefort cheese: flavour from Lactobacillus","Swiss cheese: large holes due to CO$_2$ from Saccharomyces cerevisiae; Roquefort cheese: ripened by Propionibacterium sharmanii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In Swiss cheese, the bacterium Propionibacterium sharmanii produces a large amount of CO$_2$. This gas forms the big holes.\nRoquefort cheese is ripened by growing a specific fungus on it, which gives its particular flavour.\nOption (2) swaps the two cheeses.\nOption (3) is wrong because methane comes from methanogens, which are not used in cheese making.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Bottled fruit juices bought from the market are clearer than fruit juices made at home.\nReason (R): Bottled fruit juices are clarified by treating them with lipases, which break down the fats present in the juice.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that market bottled juices are clearer than homemade ones.\nR is false. The clarification is done using pectinases and proteases, not lipases.\nLipases are used in detergent formulations to remove oily stains from laundry.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An organic farmer tries to keep insect pests at manageable levels rather than eradicate them completely.\nReason (R): If pests were eradicated, the beneficial predatory and parasitic insects that depend on them as food or hosts could not survive.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Organic farming relies on a system of checks and balances within the ecosystem, so pests are kept at manageable levels instead of being wiped out.\nR is true. NCERT notes that beneficial predators and parasites need pests as food or hosts.\nR explains A. Eradicating pests would eliminate these useful insects and weaken natural pest control, which is exactly why the farmer avoids eradication.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"A student claims: 'The gas that forms holes in Swiss cheese is the same gas that predominates in biogas from a gobar gas plant.' Which option correctly evaluates this claim?","image":"","options":["Correct, because both are produced by methanogens growing anaerobically","Incorrect, because Swiss cheese holes are formed by $CO_2$ from Propionibacterium sharmanii, while biogas is predominantly methane produced by methanogens","Correct, because both gases are mainly $CO_2$ released during fermentation","Incorrect, because Swiss cheese holes are formed by hydrogen sulphide from a fungus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In cheese making, as in dough and beverage fermentation, NCERT says the main gas produced is $CO_2$. In Swiss cheese it comes from Propionibacterium sharmanii.\nBiogas is mainly methane. It is produced by methanogens such as Methanobacterium that grow anaerobically on cellulosic material.\nSo the two gases differ, and the claim is wrong.\nHydrogen sulphide is only a minor component of biogas, and no fungus forms the holes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about BOD (biochemical oxygen demand) is correct?","image":"","options":["It is the amount of oxygen that would be consumed if all the organic matter in one litre of water were oxidised by bacteria","The higher the BOD of waste water, the lower is its polluting potential","The BOD test directly counts the number of pathogenic microbes in a water sample","Secondary treatment of sewage is continued until the BOD of the effluent rises significantly"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: this is the NCERT definition of BOD.\n(2) Incorrect: a greater BOD means more organic matter is present, so the polluting potential is higher.\n(3) Incorrect: the test measures the rate of oxygen uptake by microbes. It is therefore an indirect measure of organic matter, not a pathogen count.\n(4) Incorrect: sewage is treated until its BOD is reduced.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When larvae eat plants sprayed with Bacillus thuringiensis, the toxin is released in the larval gut and the larvae are killed.\nStatement II: Bacillus thuringiensis spray kills caterpillars as well as all other insects present on the plant.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The sprayed spores are eaten by insect larvae, and the toxin is released inside the larval gut, which kills the larvae.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that this bacterial disease kills the caterpillars but leaves other insects unharmed.\nIndiscriminate killing of useful and harmful organisms is a drawback of chemical pesticides, not of Bt.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Adding a small amount of curd to fresh, suitably warm milk helps convert the milk into curd.\nReason (R): Lactic acid bacteria break down the milk proteins themselves to form lactic acid.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The added curd acts as an inoculum or starter. It contains millions of LAB, which multiply at suitable temperatures and convert the milk to curd.\nR is false. LAB produce acids, and these acids coagulate and partially digest the milk proteins. The proteins are acted upon by the acid; they are not the source of the lactic acid.\nSo A is true and R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Toddy\n(b) Swiss cheese\n(c) Roquefort cheese\n(d) Bread\nList-II\n(i) Saccharomyces cerevisiae\n(ii) A specific fungus used for ripening\n(iii) Fermented palm sap\n(iv) Propionibacterium sharmanii","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Toddy is made by fermenting palm sap, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) Swiss cheese gets its holes from $CO_2$ made by Propionibacterium sharmanii, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) Roquefort cheese is ripened by a specific fungus, so (c)-(ii).\n(d) Bread dough is fermented by baker's yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following micro-organisms is chiefly responsible for converting milk into curd?","image":"","options":["Saccharomyces cerevisiae","Lactobacillus","Propionibacterium sharmanii","Acetobacter aceti"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: Lactobacillus and other lactic acid bacteria (LAB) grow in milk and convert it to curd.\nSaccharomyces cerevisiae is baker's or brewer's yeast. It is used for bread and alcoholic beverages, not curd.\nPropionibacterium sharmanii makes the large holes in Swiss cheese.\nAcetobacter aceti is an industrial producer of acetic acid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Acetobacter aceti, used for the commercial production of acetic acid, is a fungus.\nStatement II: Clostridium butylicum, used for the commercial production of butyric acid, is a bacterium.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Acetobacter aceti does produce acetic acid, but it is a bacterium, not a fungus. Among the NCERT acid producers, only Aspergillus niger is a fungus.\nStatement II is correct. Clostridium butylicum is a bacterium that produces butyric acid.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Leguminous plants bearing root nodules can obtain nitrogen even when the soil is poor in nitrogenous compounds.\nReason (R): Rhizobium living symbiotically in the root nodules converts atmospheric nitrogen into organic forms that the plant uses as a nutrient.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: NCERT states that Rhizobium in legume root nodules fixes atmospheric nitrogen into organic forms, which the plant uses as a nutrient.\nA is true: the plant draws on nitrogen fixed from the atmosphere, so it does not depend only on soil nitrogen compounds.\nR directly explains why A happens.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): To set curd at home, a small amount of old curd is added to fresh lukewarm milk.\nReason (R): The added curd acts as an inoculum or starter containing millions of lactic acid bacteria, which multiply at suitable temperatures and convert milk to curd.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: curd is set by adding a little curd to fresh milk.\nR is true: NCERT describes this curd as an inoculum or starter containing millions of LAB, which multiply at suitable temperatures.\nThe multiplication of these LAB is exactly what converts the milk to curd, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Alexander Fleming discovered penicillin while he was working on cultures of which bacterium?","image":"","options":["Streptococcus","Lactobacillus","Staphylococci","Clostridium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fleming was working on Staphylococci bacteria when he made the discovery.\nHe saw a mould growing on one of his unwashed culture plates, and Staphylococci could not grow around it.\nStreptococcus is linked in NCERT with streptokinase, not with penicillin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The yeast used to ferment malted cereals and fruit juices in the beverage industry, commonly called brewer's yeast, is:","image":"","options":["Monascus purpureus","Propionibacterium sharmanii","Trichoderma polysporum","Saccharomyces cerevisiae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Brewer's yeast is Saccharomyces cerevisiae. It is the same yeast that is used for bread-making.\nMonascus purpureus is a yeast that produces statins.\nPropionibacterium sharmanii is the bacterium that makes the holes in Swiss cheese.\nTrichoderma polysporum is the fungus that produces cyclosporin A.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): With reference to human beings, antibiotics are regarded as 'pro life' rather than 'against life'.\nReason (R): Antibiotics are chemicals produced by some microbes that kill or retard the growth of other, disease-causing microbes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the word means 'against life' only for disease-causing organisms. For humans, antibiotics are 'pro life'.\nR is true: this is NCERT's definition of antibiotics.\nR explains A. Antibiotics kill or check pathogens, so they protect human life rather than harm it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Cyclosporin A is administered to patients who have undergone organ transplantation.\nReason (R): Cyclosporin A is an antibiotic that kills microbes infecting the transplanted organ.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Organ-transplant patients are given cyclosporin A.\nR is false. Cyclosporin A is not used to kill microbes.\nIt is an immunosuppressive agent: it suppresses the patient's immune response so that the transplanted organ is not rejected.\nThe tempting error is to assume that every fungal product, like Penicillin, is an antibiotic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which mixture of gases is produced by bacteria in anaerobic sludge digesters of a sewage treatment plant?","image":"","options":["Oxygen, nitrogen and carbon dioxide","Methane, ammonia and oxygen","Methane, hydrogen sulphide and carbon dioxide","Ethylene, hydrogen and nitrogen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In anaerobic sludge digesters, anaerobic bacteria digest the bacteria and fungi present in the activated sludge.\nDuring this digestion they release methane, hydrogen sulphide and carbon dioxide.\nThis mixture forms biogas, which is inflammable and can be used as a source of energy.\nOxygen cannot be a product here, because the digestion is anaerobic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The large holes characteristic of 'Swiss cheese' are produced by the activity of which microbe?","image":"","options":["Lactobacillus","Propionibacterium sharmanii","Saccharomyces cerevisiae","Methanobacterium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the large holes in Swiss cheese come from a large amount of $CO_2$ produced by the bacterium Propionibacterium sharmanii.\nLactobacillus is a lactic acid bacterium that converts milk to curd. It is not linked to the holes.\nSaccharomyces cerevisiae is the yeast used for bread and beverages.\nMethanobacterium is a methanogen found in biogas production.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following fixes atmospheric nitrogen while living freely in the soil, without forming root nodules?","image":"","options":["Rhizobium","Azotobacter","Glomus","Methanobacterium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT gives Azospirillum and Azotobacter as bacteria that fix atmospheric nitrogen while free-living in the soil.\nRhizobium also fixes nitrogen, but only in symbiotic association inside legume root nodules, so it is the most tempting wrong option.\nGlomus is a mycorrhizal fungus, and Methanobacterium is a methanogen. Neither is a nitrogen fixer.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about a typical gobar gas plant is correct?","image":"","options":["Methanogens in the slurry grow aerobically on cellulosic material","The floating cover sinks steadily as gas accumulates in the tank","The spent slurry is discarded because it is toxic to crops","The spent slurry is removed through a separate outlet and can be used as fertiliser"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong: methanogens grow anaerobically.\n(2) Wrong: the floating cover rises as gas is produced.\n(3) Wrong: the spent slurry is not toxic. It is useful as a fertiliser.\n(4) Correct: NCERT says the spent slurry is removed through another outlet and may be used as fertiliser.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"A villager tries to 'improve' his gobar gas plant by removing the cover and continuously bubbling air through the dung slurry. What is the most likely result?","image":"","options":["Methane output rises, because aeration increases microbial activity as in sewage aeration tanks","Methane output falls sharply, because methanogens grow only under anaerobic conditions","Methane output is unchanged, but the spent slurry can no longer be used as fertiliser","The slurry starts producing ethanol instead of methane, because yeast takes over"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Methanogens such as Methanobacterium produce methane only when they grow anaerobically on cellulosic material.\nBubbling air through the slurry destroys these anaerobic conditions, so methane production falls sharply.\nOption (1) is the tempting trap: aeration tanks support aerobic flocs that reduce BOD, not methane formation.\nNCERT notes that biogas forms in anaerobic sludge digesters, not in aerated tanks.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"NCERT lists the following as microbes: protozoa, bacteria, fungi, viruses, viroids and prions. Which option correctly identifies ALL the members of this list that have a eukaryotic cellular organisation?","image":"","options":["Protozoa and fungi","Bacteria and fungi","Protozoa, bacteria and fungi","Fungi only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Viruses, viroids and prions are acellular, so they are not cellular at all.\nBacteria are cellular but prokaryotic.\nProtozoa and fungi are eukaryotic cells, belonging to Kingdoms Protista and Fungi.\nOption (3) wrongly includes bacteria. Option (4) leaves out protozoa.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, microbes are found even deep inside geysers (thermal vents). Up to what temperature can such sites reach?","image":"","options":["About 60°C","About 80°C","As high as 100°C","As high as 150°C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that microbes live where no other life form could exist.\nOne example is deep inside geysers (thermal vents), where the temperature may be as high as 100°C.\nThe values 60°C and 80°C are too low for what the text states. 150°C goes beyond it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): After secondary treatment, a small part of the activated sludge is pumped back into the aeration tank.\nReason (R): The returned activated sludge serves as an inoculum of flocs for treating fresh primary effluent.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Activated sludge is the sediment of bacterial flocs in the settling tank, and a small part of it is pumped back into the aeration tank.\nR is true. This returned portion acts as the inoculum, supplying ready microbes for the incoming effluent.\nR therefore explains why A is done. The major remaining part of the sludge goes to anaerobic sludge digesters.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The effluent leaving the aeration tank has a much lower BOD than the primary effluent entering it.\nReason (R): Aerobic microbes growing as flocs in the aeration tank consume the major part of the organic matter in the effluent.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the purpose of secondary treatment is to reduce the BOD significantly.\nR is true: as the flocs grow, the aerobic microbes consume most of the organic matter.\nBOD indirectly measures the organic matter in water. Removing organic matter therefore lowers BOD, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"A free-living fungus that is very common in root ecosystems is being developed as a biological control agent against several plant pathogens. Which fungus is it?","image":"","options":["Glomus","Trichoderma","Aspergillus niger","Penicillium notatum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes Trichoderma species as free-living fungi common in root ecosystems. They are effective biocontrol agents of several plant pathogens.\nGlomus is the tempting wrong option. It also lives with roots, but it forms mycorrhiza and helps the plant absorb phosphorus, so it is a biofertiliser, not a biocontrol agent.\nAspergillus niger is used to produce citric acid.\nPenicillium notatum is the source of penicillin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (microbial product) with List-II (its use).\nList-I:\nA. Streptokinase\nB. Cyclosporin A\nC. Statins\nD. Pectinases\nList-II:\n(i) Clarification of bottled fruit juices\n(ii) Lowering blood cholesterol\n(iii) Removing clots from blood vessels after myocardial infarction\n(iv) Suppressing immune response in organ-transplant patients\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Streptokinase comes from Streptococcus. It is a 'clot buster' that removes clots after a heart attack.\nB-(iv): Cyclosporin A is an immunosuppressant used in organ-transplant patients.\nC-(ii): Statins lower blood cholesterol.\nD-(i): Pectinases, along with proteases, clarify bottled fruit juices.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"A typical biogas plant has a concrete tank 10-15 feet deep, fed with a slurry of dung. A cover placed over the slurry keeps rising, and the tank has two separate outlets. Which interpretation of this setup is correct?","image":"","options":["The cover rises because aerobic bacteria release oxygen; one outlet supplies oxygen to houses","The cover rises as methanogens produce gas anaerobically; one outlet pipes biogas to houses and the other removes spent slurry that can be used as fertiliser","The cover rises because yeast ferments the dung to ethanol vapour; the spent slurry is toxic and must be discarded","The cover rises as gas is produced; the spent slurry is pumped back into the aeration tank of a sewage plant as inoculum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The floating cover rises as gas builds up from microbial activity. Methanogens grow anaerobically on cellulosic material in the dung.\nOne outlet connects to a pipe that supplies biogas to nearby houses.\nThe other outlet removes the spent slurry, which can be used as fertiliser.\nOxygen (1) and ethanol (3) are not produced here.\nReturning sludge as inoculum (4) belongs to the sewage aeration tank, not the biogas plant.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The technology of biogas production in India was developed mainly through the efforts of:","image":"","options":["ICMR and KVIC","IARI and NDRI","IARI and KVIC","CSIR and NABARD"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT credits biogas technology in India mainly to the Indian Agricultural Research Institute (IARI) and the Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC).\nThe other pairs either name a medical or dairy institute or omit KVIC.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Streptococcus, the source of streptokinase, is a bacterium.\n(ii) Cyclosporin A helps prevent rejection of transplanted organs.\n(iii) Statins competitively inhibit an enzyme needed for cholesterol synthesis.\n(iv) Cyclosporin A is produced by Monascus purpureus.\n(v) Streptokinase is used as a blood-cholesterol lowering agent.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Streptococcus is a bacterium.\n(ii) Correct: cyclosporin A is an immunosuppressant used in organ-transplant patients.\n(iii) Correct: this is the mechanism of statins given in NCERT.\n(iv) Incorrect: cyclosporin A comes from Trichoderma polysporum. Monascus purpureus produces statins.\n(v) Incorrect: streptokinase is a clot buster. Statins are the cholesterol-lowering agents.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Wine and whisky are different drinks even though both are made by yeast fermentation.\nReason (R): The type of alcoholic drink obtained depends on the raw material fermented and on whether the fermented broth is distilled or not.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Both drinks come from yeast fermentation, yet they are different products.\nR is true. NCERT says that the raw material and the type of processing, with or without distillation, decide which drink is obtained.\nR explains A. Wine is undistilled, while whisky is made by distilling the fermented broth.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The organism used for the commercial production of ethanol is:","image":"","options":["Acetobacter aceti","Lactobacillus","Aspergillus niger","Saccharomyces cerevisiae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae ferments sugars to ethanol. NCERT names it for the commercial production of ethanol.\nThe other options produce organic acids instead: acetic acid, lactic acid and citric acid respectively.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statins, which have been commercialised as blood-cholesterol lowering agents, are produced by:","image":"","options":["Trichoderma polysporum","Aspergillus niger","Penicillium notatum","Monascus purpureus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, statins are produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus.\nTrichoderma polysporum produces cyclosporin A.\nAspergillus niger produces citric acid.\nPenicillium notatum produces Penicillin.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Baculoviruses are especially desirable for pest control in an integrated pest management programme or in ecologically sensitive areas.\nReason (R): Baculoviruses of the genus Nucleopolyhedrovirus are species-specific, narrow-spectrum insecticides that show no negative impact on plants, mammals, birds, fish or non-target insects.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT recommends baculoviruses where beneficial insects must be conserved or where sensitive areas are being treated.\nR is true. These viruses act only on target species and do not harm non-target organisms.\nThis narrow-spectrum safety is exactly why they suit IPM, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Rhizobium and Azospirillum both increase the nitrogen available to plants, but only Rhizobium needs a symbiotic association with the host to do so.\nStatement II: In mycorrhiza, Glomus fixes atmospheric nitrogen and also adds organic matter to the soil, like blue-green algae.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: Rhizobium fixes nitrogen in symbiosis within legume root nodules. Azospirillum fixes nitrogen while free-living in the soil.\nStatement II is incorrect: Glomus absorbs phosphorus from the soil and passes it to the plant. It does not fix atmospheric nitrogen.\nAdding organic matter to the soil is a feature NCERT attributes to blue-green algae (cyanobacteria), not to Glomus.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The word 'antibiotic' is formed from two Greek words. What is its literal meaning?","image":"","options":["For life","Against life","Against disease","Product of life"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"'Anti' is a Greek word meaning 'against', and 'bio' means 'life'.\nTogether, 'antibiotic' literally means 'against life'.\nThis refers to disease-causing organisms. For human beings, antibiotics are actually 'pro life'.\n'Against disease' describes what they do, but it is not the literal meaning of the word.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Under the Ganga Action Plan and Yamuna Action Plan initiated by the Ministry of Environment and Forests, the main proposal to save these rivers from pollution was to:","image":"","options":["Build a large number of sewage treatment plants so that only treated sewage is discharged into the rivers","Build biogas plants along the river banks to use cattle dung","Introduce methanogenic bacteria directly into the river water","Divert all municipal sewage into the sea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Untreated sewage is often discharged directly into rivers. This pollutes them and increases water-borne diseases.\nUnder these action plans, the proposal is to build many sewage treatment plants (STPs). Only treated sewage would then reach the rivers.\nBiogas plants and methanogens are not the stated means of these plans.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"'Toddy', a traditional drink of some parts of southern India, is prepared by fermenting:","image":"","options":["Malted barley","Sap from palms","Fruit juice of grapes","Rice dough"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes toddy as a traditional drink of some parts of southern India. It is made by fermenting sap from palms.\nMalted cereals and fruit juices are fermented by brewer's yeast to make beverages such as beer and wine, not toddy.\nRice-based dough is fermented to make foods like idli and dosa.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Trichoderma, which is being developed as a biological control for plant diseases, is best described as:","image":"","options":["A free-living fungus common in root ecosystems that acts against several plant pathogens","A virus that specifically attacks insects and other arthropods","A bacterium whose spores are sprayed on brassicas to kill caterpillars","A predatory insect that feeds on aphids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT describes Trichoderma species as free-living fungi that are very common in root ecosystems.\nThey are effective biocontrol agents of several plant pathogens.\nOption (2) describes baculoviruses.\nOption (3) describes Bacillus thuringiensis.\nOption (4) describes the ladybird.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Conversion of milk into curd by lactic acid bacteria improves its nutritional quality mainly by increasing the content of","image":"","options":["vitamin C","vitamin D","vitamin $B_{12}$","vitamin A"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that LAB convert milk to curd and in doing so improve its nutritional quality by increasing vitamin $B_{12}$.\nNone of the other vitamins listed is mentioned as being enriched by LAB activity.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"During primary treatment of sewage, the grit (soil and small pebbles) is removed by:","image":"","options":["Sequential filtration","Sedimentation","Aeration with constant agitation","Anaerobic digestion"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Primary treatment removes particles physically and in stages.\nFirst, floating debris is removed by sequential filtration.\nThen grit (soil and small pebbles) is removed by sedimentation.\nFiltration is the tempting wrong option, but it removes only the floating debris.\nAeration and anaerobic digestion are biological steps of secondary treatment.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Whisky, brandy and rum differ from wine and beer in the processing step that follows fermentation by yeast.\nReason (R): Whisky, brandy and rum are obtained by distilling the fermented broth, whereas wine and beer are produced without distillation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Brewer's yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) ferments malted cereals and fruit juices to ethanol for all of these drinks. The drinks then differ in how the fermented broth is processed.\nR is true. Distillation is used for whisky, brandy and rum. Wine and beer are not distilled.\nR states the exact processing difference that A refers to, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (microbe) with List-II (organic acid produced commercially).\nList-I:\nA. Aspergillus niger\nB. Acetobacter aceti\nC. Clostridium butylicum\nD. Lactobacillus\nList-II:\nI. Lactic acid\nII. Butyric acid\nIII. Citric acid\nIV. Acetic acid","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Aspergillus niger is a fungus that produces citric acid (III).\nB. Acetobacter aceti is a bacterium that produces acetic acid (IV).\nC. Clostridium butylicum is a bacterium that produces butyric acid (II).\nD. Lactobacillus is a bacterium that produces lactic acid (I).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Detergent formulations that are good at removing oily stains from laundry contain which microbial enzymes?","image":"","options":["Pectinases","Proteases","Lipases","Streptokinases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Lipases act on lipids (fats and oils), so NCERT notes their use in detergents to remove oily stains.\nPectinases and proteases are used to clarify fruit juices.\nStreptokinase is a clot buster.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which group of microbes serves as an important biofertiliser in paddy (rice) fields?","image":"","options":["Mycorrhizal fungi","Cyanobacteria","Methanogens","Lactic acid bacteria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT specifically states that cyanobacteria serve as an important biofertiliser in paddy fields.\nMany of them, such as Anabaena and Nostoc, fix atmospheric nitrogen and also add organic matter to the soil.\nMycorrhizal fungi help with phosphorus uptake but are not the biofertiliser highlighted for paddy fields.\nMethanogens and lactic acid bacteria are not biofertilisers.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following pairs are correctly matched?\n(i) Lactobacillus (bacterium) – lactic acid\n(ii) Clostridium butylicum (fungus) – butyric acid\n(iii) Saccharomyces cerevisiae – commercial production of ethanol\n(iv) Pectinases – removal of oily stains from laundry\n(v) Aspergillus niger – acetic acid\n(vi) Proteases – clarification of bottled fruit juices","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Lactobacillus is a bacterium that produces lactic acid.\n(ii) Incorrect: Clostridium butylicum is a bacterium, not a fungus.\n(iii) Correct: yeast is used for commercial ethanol.\n(iv) Incorrect: lipases remove oily stains; pectinases help clarify juices.\n(v) Incorrect: Aspergillus niger produces citric acid; acetic acid comes from Acetobacter aceti.\n(vi) Correct: proteases, along with pectinases, clarify bottled juices.\nThree pairs are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Trichoderma polysporum, the source of cyclosporin A, is a bacterium.\nStatement II: Streptokinase is used to remove clots from the blood vessels of patients who have undergone myocardial infarction.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. Trichoderma polysporum is a fungus, not a bacterium. The bacterium among these producers is Streptococcus.\nStatement II is true. Streptokinase acts as a clot buster in patients after myocardial infarction.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Bottled fruit juices bought from the market are clearer than juices made at home.\nReason (R): Bottled juices are treated with lipases that break down the fats causing cloudiness.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that bottled juices are clearer than homemade ones.\nR is false. The clarification is done with pectinases and proteases, not lipases.\nLipases are used in detergents to remove oily stains.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair of alcoholic beverages is produced by distillation of the fermented broth?","image":"","options":["Wine and beer","Whisky and rum","Beer and brandy","Wine and whisky"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Brewer's yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) ferments malted cereals and fruit juices to produce ethanol.\nWine and beer are made without distillation.\nWhisky, brandy and rum are made by distilling the fermented broth.\nOnly option (2) lists two distilled drinks. Options (3) and (4) each mix one undistilled drink with one distilled drink.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"'Roquefort cheese' gets its particular flavour because it is:","image":"","options":["Ripened by growing a specific fungus on it","Fermented by lactic acid bacteria only","Filled with $CO_2$ released by a bacterium","Treated with pectinases and proteases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that Roquefort cheese is ripened by growing a specific fungus on it, and this gives its particular flavour.\n$CO_2$ released by a bacterium explains the holes of Swiss cheese, not the flavour of Roquefort.\nPectinases and proteases are used to clarify bottled fruit juices.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Cattle dung (gobar) is a suitable raw material for generating biogas.\nReason (R): Methanogens live in the rumen of cattle, where they help break down the cellulose present in cattle food.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: dung is fed as slurry in gobar gas plants.\nR is true: methanogens in the rumen help digest cellulose and contribute to cattle nutrition.\nBecause these bacteria live in the rumen, cattle excreta is rich in methanogens.\nThat richness is exactly why dung is used for biogas, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (microbe) with List-II (organic acid it is used to produce commercially).\nList-I:\n(A) Aspergillus niger\n(B) Acetobacter aceti\n(C) Clostridium butylicum\n(D) Lactobacillus\nList-II:\n(i) Lactic acid\n(ii) Butyric acid\n(iii) Citric acid\n(iv) Acetic acid","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Aspergillus niger, a fungus, produces citric acid: A-iii.\n(B) Acetobacter aceti, a bacterium, produces acetic acid: B-iv.\n(C) Clostridium butylicum, a bacterium, produces butyric acid: C-ii.\n(D) Lactobacillus, a bacterium, produces lactic acid: D-i.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The immunosuppressive agent cyclosporin A, given to organ-transplant patients, is commercially obtained from which microbe?","image":"","options":["Trichoderma polysporum","Monascus purpureus","Streptococcus","Penicillium notatum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that cyclosporin A is produced by the fungus Trichoderma polysporum.\nIt suppresses the immune response, so the body is less likely to reject a transplanted organ.\nMonascus purpureus is the tempting wrong option, but it is the yeast that produces statins.\nStreptococcus produces streptokinase, and Penicillium notatum produces penicillin.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about secondary (biological) treatment of sewage are correct?\n(i) The primary effluent is passed into large aeration tanks.\n(ii) The effluent in aeration tanks is constantly agitated mechanically and air is pumped into it.\n(iii) Microbes in flocs consume the major part of the organic matter of the effluent.\n(iv) Once the BOD is reduced significantly, the effluent is passed into a settling tank.\n(v) Organic matter in the aeration tank is degraded mainly by anaerobic methanogens.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the primary effluent enters the aeration tanks.\n(ii) Correct: mechanical agitation and air pumping allow vigorous aerobic growth.\n(iii) Correct: the flocs consume most of the organic matter, which lowers the BOD.\n(iv) Correct: the effluent goes to a settling tank where the flocs sediment.\n(v) Incorrect: aeration tanks support aerobic microbes. Methanogens work later, in the anaerobic sludge digesters.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Baculoviruses are especially desirable when beneficial insects are being conserved in an integrated pest management (IPM) programme.\nReason (R): Baculoviruses are species-specific, narrow-spectrum agents with no negative impact on plants, mammals, birds, fish or non-target insects.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that baculoviruses are desirable in IPM programmes where beneficial insects are being conserved, and in ecologically sensitive areas.\nR is true. They are narrow-spectrum, species-specific agents that do not harm non-target organisms.\nR explains A: because only the target pest is affected, beneficial insects survive.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"When brewer's yeast ferments malted cereals and fruit juices, the main alcohol it produces is:","image":"","options":["Ethanol","Methanol","Butanol","Glycerol"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that Saccharomyces cerevisiae ferments malted cereals and fruit juices to produce ethanol.\nThe same yeast is also used for commercial production of ethanol.\nMethanol, butanol and glycerol are not the alcohol produced in beverage fermentation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Penicillin was named after the organism that produces it. This organism is:","image":"","options":["Penicillium notatum, a bacterium","Penicillium notatum, a mould (fungus)","Trichoderma polysporum, a fungus","Aspergillus niger, a mould"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Fleming found that a chemical produced by a mould stopped bacteria from growing.\nHe named this chemical Penicillin after the mould Penicillium notatum.\nPenicillium is a fungus, not a bacterium, so option (1) is wrong.\nTrichoderma polysporum produces cyclosporin A, and Aspergillus niger produces citric acid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Swiss cheese shows large holes in its body.\nReason (R): Propionibacterium sharmanii produces a large amount of carbon dioxide during the making of Swiss cheese.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: Swiss cheese is known for its large holes.\nR is true: Propionibacterium sharmanii releases a large amount of $CO_2$.\nThis gas gets trapped in the cheese and forms the holes, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements about the discovery of penicillin is correct?","image":"","options":["Penicillin was obtained from a bacterium that had contaminated Fleming's Staphylococcus culture plate.","Penicillin was discovered through a planned screening of many moulds for antibacterial activity.","Chain and Florey, not Fleming, established penicillin's full potential as an effective antibiotic, and all three shared the 1945 Nobel Prize.","Penicillin is called 'against life' because it kills microbes that live in the human body."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: The contaminant was a mould, Penicillium notatum, not a bacterium.\n(2) Incorrect: Penicillin was a chance discovery made on an unwashed plate, not the result of planned screening.\n(3) Correct: Fleming discovered and named penicillin. Chain and Florey later established its potential. All three received the Nobel Prize in 1945.\n(4) Incorrect: 'Against life' refers to disease-causing organisms. For humans, antibiotics are pro life.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Rhizobium\n(B) Azospirillum\n(C) Glomus\n(D) Anabaena\nList-II\n(i) Autotrophic nitrogen fixer used in paddy fields\n(ii) Fungus that passes phosphorus to the plant\n(iii) Nitrogen fixation in legume root nodules\n(iv) Free-living nitrogen fixer in soil\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Rhizobium forms symbiotic root nodules in legumes and fixes nitrogen there.\nB-(iv): Azospirillum fixes nitrogen while free-living in the soil.\nC-(ii): Glomus forms mycorrhiza and passes absorbed phosphorus to the plant.\nD-(i): Anabaena is an autotrophic cyanobacterium that fixes nitrogen in paddy fields.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Three patients are treated with microbial bioactive molecules:\nP – a clot blocking a coronary artery after a heart attack\nQ – a recently transplanted kidney\nR – high blood cholesterol\nWhich option correctly gives the molecule for each patient along with the type of microbe that produces it?","image":"","options":["P – streptokinase (fungus); Q – cyclosporin A (bacterium); R – statin (yeast)","P – streptokinase (bacterium); Q – cyclosporin A (fungus); R – statin (yeast)","P – statin (yeast); Q – cyclosporin A (fungus); R – streptokinase (bacterium)","P – streptokinase (bacterium); Q – statin (yeast); R – cyclosporin A (fungus)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"P needs a clot buster. That is streptokinase, produced by the bacterium Streptococcus.\nQ needs immunosuppression. That is cyclosporin A, produced by the fungus Trichoderma polysporum.\nR needs cholesterol lowering. That is a statin, produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus.\nOption (1) has the right drugs but swaps the producer types for P and Q.\nOptions (3) and (4) assign drugs to the wrong patients.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Citric acid\n(b) Commercial ethanol\n(c) Removal of oily stains from laundry\n(d) Clarification of bottled fruit juices\nList-II\n(i) Pectinases and proteases\n(ii) Lipases\n(iii) Saccharomyces cerevisiae\n(iv) Aspergillus niger","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Citric acid is produced by Aspergillus niger, so (a)-(iv).\n(b) Ethanol is produced by Saccharomyces cerevisiae, so (b)-(iii).\n(c) Lipases in detergents remove oily stains, so (c)-(ii).\n(d) Pectinases and proteases clarify fruit juices, so (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are sites where NCERT states that microbes are present?\nSoil; air; inside the bodies of animals and plants; deep inside thermal vents; under layers of snow several metres thick; highly acidic environments","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT says microbes are present everywhere: in soil, water, air, and inside our bodies and those of other animals and plants.\nThey are also found at extreme sites: deep inside thermal vents, deep in the soil, under snow several metres thick, and in highly acidic environments.\nAll six listed sites are therefore correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match the bioactive molecule in List-I with its microbial source in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Streptokinase\n(B) Cyclosporin A\n(C) Statins\n(D) Penicillin\nList-II:\n(i) Monascus purpureus\n(ii) Penicillium notatum\n(iii) Streptococcus\n(iv) Trichoderma polysporum","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) Streptokinase, the clot buster, comes from the bacterium Streptococcus: A-iii.\n(B) Cyclosporin A, an immunosuppressant, comes from the fungus Trichoderma polysporum: B-iv.\n(C) Statins, which lower blood cholesterol, come from the yeast Monascus purpureus: C-i.\n(D) Penicillin is named after the mould Penicillium notatum: D-ii.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"In NCERT's description of microbial diversity, prions are described as:","image":"","options":["Free, circular RNA molecules without a protein coat","Proteinaceous infectious agents","DNA viruses that lack a capsid","Prokaryotic cells that lack a cell wall"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists prions among the microbes and calls them proteinaceous infectious agents.\nSo they are made of protein, not nucleic acid.\nOption (1) describes viroids, which are free RNA without a protein coat. It is the most tempting wrong choice.\nOptions (3) and (4) do not describe prions at all.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about biogas are correct?\n(a) Methanogens grow anaerobically on cellulosic material.\n(b) Methanobacterium is found in the rumen of cattle as well as in anaerobic sludge.\n(c) Biogas consists predominantly of carbon dioxide.\n(d) The floating cover over the slurry keeps rising as gas is produced.\n(e) The spent slurry of a biogas plant can be used as fertiliser.\n(f) Biogas technology in India was developed mainly through the efforts of ICAR and NBRI.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct. Methanogens produce methane, $CO_2$ and $H_2$ from cellulosic material.\n(b) Correct. Methanobacterium occurs in both the rumen and anaerobic sludge.\n(c) Incorrect. Biogas is predominantly methane.\n(d) Correct.\n(e) Correct.\n(f) Incorrect. The technology was developed by IARI and KVIC.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"A student boils milk and, without letting it cool, immediately stirs in a spoonful of curd as starter. Even after many hours the milk does not set into curd. The most likely reason is that","image":"","options":["boiling destroys the vitamin $B_{12}$ that LAB need to produce acid","the high temperature killed or inactivated the lactic acid bacteria present in the starter","heated milk proteins can no longer be coagulated by acids","the $CO_2$ needed for curd formation escaped from the hot milk"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Curd forms only when the LAB in the starter multiply at a suitable temperature and produce acids that coagulate milk proteins.\nVery hot milk kills or inactivates these LAB, so no acid is produced and no curd forms.\nOption (1) is wrong: LAB increase vitamin $B_{12}$; they do not require it from milk.\nOption (3) is wrong: boiled milk sets normally once cooled.\nOption (4) is wrong: curd formation depends on acid, not $CO_2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are microbial biocontrol agents?\nLadybird, Dragonfly, Bacillus thuringiensis, Trichoderma, Nucleopolyhedrovirus, Rhizobium, Methanobacterium","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ladybird and Dragonfly are insects, so they are biocontrol agents but not microbes.\nBacillus thuringiensis is a bacterium that controls caterpillars, so it counts.\nTrichoderma is a fungus that controls plant pathogens, so it counts.\nNucleopolyhedrovirus is a baculovirus that controls insects, so it counts.\nRhizobium is a biofertiliser and Methanobacterium is a methanogen used in biogas, so neither counts.\nTotal = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of sewage treatment in the correct sequence:\nP. Removal of grit (soil and small pebbles) by sedimentation\nQ. Removal of floating debris by sequential filtration\nR. Growth of aerobic flocs that consume organic matter in aeration tanks\nS. Sedimentation of flocs to form activated sludge\nT. Digestion of the major part of the sludge by anaerobic bacteria, releasing biogas","image":"","options":["Q → P → R → S → T","P → Q → R → S → T","Q → P → S → R → T","Q → R → P → S → T"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Primary treatment comes first. Floating debris is removed by sequential filtration (Q), and then grit is removed by sedimentation (P).\nSecondary treatment follows. Flocs grow in the aeration tanks and lower the BOD (R).\nThe effluent then goes to a settling tank, where the flocs sediment as activated sludge (S).\nThe major part of this sludge is digested anaerobically in sludge digesters, producing biogas (T).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Bread dough is fermented by Saccharomyces cerevisiae, the same species that is called brewer's yeast in beverage production.\n(ii) The fermentation of idli and dosa dough is carried out by bacteria.\n(iii) The acids produced by lactic acid bacteria completely digest milk proteins into free amino acids.\n(iv) Lactic acid bacteria present in our stomach help keep disease-causing microbes in check.\n(v) Bread dough rises because yeast releases oxygen during fermentation.\n(vi) Curd is nutritionally richer than milk in vitamin $B_{12}$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: baker's yeast and brewer's yeast are the same species, S. cerevisiae.\n(ii) Correct: idli and dosa dough is fermented by bacteria.\n(iii) Incorrect: the acids coagulate the milk proteins and digest them only partially, not completely.\n(iv) Correct: LAB in the stomach check pathogens.\n(v) Incorrect: dough rises because of $CO_2$, not oxygen.\n(vi) Correct: curd formation increases vitamin $B_{12}$.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"A patient has suffered a myocardial infarction caused by a clot blocking a coronary artery. Which microbial product is used to remove such clots?","image":"","options":["Streptokinase","Cyclosporin A","Statin","Lipase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Streptokinase comes from Streptococcus and is modified by genetic engineering. It is used as a 'clot buster' to remove clots from the blood vessels of patients who have had a myocardial infarction.\nStatins lower cholesterol but do not dissolve an existing clot.\nCyclosporin A is an immunosuppressant.\nLipases are used in detergents to remove oily stains.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"In which year were Fleming, Chain and Florey awarded the Nobel Prize for the discovery of penicillin?","image":"","options":["1928","1939","1945","1953"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that Fleming, Chain and Florey shared the Nobel Prize in 1945.\nThis came after penicillin had been widely used during World War II.\nThe other years are not linked with this award in the chapter.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"A student repeats Fleming's observation. A Petri dish has a lawn of Staphylococci, and a mould colony grows in one region. A clear zone with no bacterial growth forms around the mould, even where the mould has not spread. Which explanation best fits this observation, as interpreted by Fleming?","image":"","options":["The mould used up all the nutrients in the medium, starving the bacteria everywhere on the plate","The mould physically covered the bacteria and blocked their oxygen supply","A chemical released by the mould diffused into the medium and inhibited bacterial growth around it","Contaminants on the unwashed plate killed the bacteria only near the mould"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The bacteria fail to grow even where the mould has not spread. So simple physical covering cannot explain the clear zone.\nNutrient exhaustion would affect the whole plate, not just a ring around the mould.\nFleming concluded that a chemical produced by the mould caused the effect, and he named it penicillin.\nA substance diffusing outward from the mould explains a clear zone centred on it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following alcoholic beverages is produced WITHOUT distillation of the fermented broth?","image":"","options":["Whisky","Rum","Beer","Brandy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT divides alcoholic drinks by whether the fermented broth is distilled.\nWine and beer are made without distillation.\nWhisky, brandy and rum are made by distilling the fermented broth.\nSo beer is the only option made without distillation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Industrial-scale production of beverages and antibiotics requires growing microbes in very large vessels. What are these vessels called?","image":"","options":["Aeration tanks","Fermentors","Anaerobic sludge digesters","Petri dishes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that industrial-scale production needs microbes to be grown in very large vessels called fermentors.\nAeration tanks and anaerobic sludge digesters are parts of a sewage treatment plant, not production vessels.\nPetri dishes hold small laboratory cultures that form colonies. They cannot support industrial-scale growth.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following microbes is used for the commercial production of butyric acid?","image":"","options":["Acetobacter aceti","Clostridium butylicum","Aspergillus niger","Lactobacillus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists Clostridium butylicum, a bacterium, as the producer of butyric acid. The name 'butylicum' is a helpful memory clue.\nAcetobacter aceti produces acetic acid.\nAspergillus niger, a fungus, produces citric acid.\nLactobacillus produces lactic acid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (microbial product) with List-II (its use or action):\nList-I\nA. Streptokinase\nB. Cyclosporin A\nC. Statins\nD. Lipases\nList-II\nI. Removal of oily stains in detergents\nII. Competitive inhibition of the enzyme for cholesterol synthesis\nIII. Dissolving clots in blood vessels\nIV. Immunosuppression in organ-transplant patients","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Streptokinase from Streptococcus, modified by genetic engineering, is a 'clot buster' given after myocardial infarction. A-III.\nB. Cyclosporin A from Trichoderma polysporum is an immunosuppressant for organ-transplant patients. B-IV.\nC. Statins from Monascus purpureus lower blood cholesterol by competitively inhibiting the enzyme that synthesises it. C-II.\nD. Lipases are added to detergents to remove oily stains. D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (food/product) with List-II (microbial basis):\nList-I\n(a) Toddy\n(b) Large holes in Swiss cheese\n(c) Roquefort cheese\n(d) Bread dough\nList-II\n(i) Ripened by growing a specific fungus on it\n(ii) Made by fermenting sap from palms\n(iii) Large amount of $CO_2$ released by Propionibacterium sharmanii\n(iv) Fermented by Saccharomyces cerevisiae\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Toddy, a traditional drink of southern India, is made by fermenting palm sap: (ii).\n(b) The large holes in Swiss cheese form because Propionibacterium sharmanii releases a large amount of $CO_2$: (iii).\n(c) Roquefort cheese gets its flavour from a specific fungus grown on it during ripening: (i).\n(d) Bread dough is fermented by baker's yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae: (iv).\nOption (2) is the tempting distractor. It swaps (b) and (c), but in Swiss cheese the holes come from bacterial gas production, not from fungal ripening.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"In a sewage treatment plant, the air pumps of the aeration tanks break down, and the primary effluent stands in the tanks without aeration. Which outcome is most likely?","image":"","options":["The BOD of the effluent would remain high because useful aerobic microbes could not grow vigorously into flocs and consume the organic matter","The BOD would fall faster because methanogens would convert the organic matter into biogas within the aeration tank","Grit and small pebbles would fail to settle, so primary sludge would not form","There would be no effect, because primary treatment already removes all dissolved organic matter"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Aeration (air pumping plus agitation) allows vigorous growth of aerobic microbes into flocs, and these flocs consume most of the organic matter.\nWithout aeration, little organic matter would be oxidised, so the BOD would stay high.\nOption (2) fails because biogas production is assigned to separate anaerobic digesters and does not serve to reduce the effluent's BOD.\nOption (3) fails because grit settles during primary treatment.\nOption (4) fails because primary treatment is only physical and leaves organic matter in the effluent.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are cyanobacteria that NCERT cites as nitrogen fixers?\nAnabaena, Azotobacter, Nostoc, Glomus, Oscillatoria, Rhizobium","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Anabaena: a nitrogen-fixing cyanobacterium. Counted.\nNostoc: a nitrogen-fixing cyanobacterium. Counted.\nOscillatoria: a nitrogen-fixing cyanobacterium. Counted.\nAzotobacter: fixes nitrogen, but it is a free-living bacterium, not a cyanobacterium. Not counted.\nRhizobium: a symbiotic bacterium, not a cyanobacterium. Not counted.\nGlomus: a mycorrhizal fungus. Not counted.\nTotal = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Large vessels used to grow microbes for the production of beverages and antibiotics on an industrial scale are called:","image":"","options":["Bioreactors of the anaerobic sludge type","Aeration tanks","Fermentors","Sludge digesters"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that industrial-scale production requires growing microbes in very large vessels called fermentors.\nAeration tanks are used in secondary treatment of sewage, not for making beverages.\nSludge digesters (anaerobic sludge digesters) also belong to sewage treatment. In them, activated sludge is digested and biogas is produced.\nOption (1) is a made-up term that mixes ideas from sewage treatment.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of sewage treatment in the correct sequence:\nA. Removal of grit by sedimentation\nB. Agitation of effluent with air pumped into aeration tanks\nC. Removal of floating debris by sequential filtration\nD. Settling of bacterial flocs to form activated sludge\nE. Digestion of sludge by anaerobic bacteria, releasing biogas","image":"","options":["A, C, B, D, E","C, A, D, B, E","C, B, A, D, E","C, A, B, D, E"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Primary treatment: floating debris is removed first by sequential filtration (C), then grit by sedimentation (A).\nSecondary treatment: the primary effluent is aerated so that flocs grow and lower the BOD (B).\nThe flocs then settle in a settling tank as activated sludge (D).\nMost of this sludge goes to anaerobic sludge digesters, where gases forming biogas are produced (E).\nSequence: C, A, B, D, E.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following organisms used in the industrial production of chemicals are bacteria?\nAspergillus niger, Acetobacter aceti, Clostridium butylicum, Lactobacillus, Saccharomyces cerevisiae","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Aspergillus niger is a fungus.\nAcetobacter aceti is a bacterium.\nClostridium butylicum is a bacterium.\nLactobacillus is a bacterium.\nSaccharomyces cerevisiae is a yeast, which is a fungus.\nSo three of the five are bacteria.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The yeast used for bread-making is the same species used as brewer's yeast.\n(ii) The dough for idli and dosa is fermented by baker's yeast added to it.\n(iii) LAB produce acids during their growth in milk.\n(iv) Curd contains less vitamin $B_{12}$ than the milk from which it is made.\n(v) LAB in our stomach help check disease-causing microbes.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Saccharomyces cerevisiae serves as both baker's yeast and brewer's yeast.\n(ii) Incorrect: NCERT states that idli and dosa dough is fermented by bacteria.\n(iii) Correct: LAB produce acids that coagulate milk proteins.\n(iv) Incorrect: curd formation increases vitamin $B_{12}$.\n(v) Correct: LAB check pathogens in the stomach.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Most baculoviruses used as species-specific, narrow-spectrum biological control agents against insects belong to which genus?","image":"","options":["Nucleopolyhedrovirus","Methanobacterium","Bacillus","Rhizobium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Baculoviruses are pathogens that attack insects and other arthropods. NCERT states that most baculoviruses used in biocontrol belong to the genus Nucleopolyhedrovirus.\nBacillus is tempting because Bacillus thuringiensis is also a biocontrol agent. However, it is a bacterium, not a virus.\nMethanobacterium is a methanogen that produces biogas.\nRhizobium is a nitrogen-fixing symbiont of legume roots.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Different varieties of cheese are recognised by their characteristic texture, flavour and taste. According to NCERT, this specificity mainly comes from:","image":"","options":["The breed of animal whose milk is used","The microbes used in making the cheese","The amount of salt added during processing","The temperature at which milk is boiled"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that cheese is one of the oldest foods made using microbes.\nThe distinct texture, flavour and taste of each variety come from the specific microbes used, for example Propionibacterium in Swiss cheese and a fungus in Roquefort.\nThe other options are not given by NCERT as the source of this specificity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Biogas plants are more often built in rural areas of India.\nReason (R): Cattle dung, which is rich in methanogenic bacteria, is available in large quantities in rural areas.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that biogas plants are more often built in rural areas, where the gas is used for cooking and lighting.\nR is true: methanogens live in the cattle rumen, so dung (gobar) is rich in them. Cattle dung is plentiful in villages.\nR explains A: the raw material for biogas is readily available in villages.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Roquefort cheese acquires its particular flavour from a bacterium grown on it during ripening.\nStatement II: Cheese is one of the oldest food items in whose preparation microbes were used.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect: Roquefort cheese is ripened by growing a specific fungus, not a bacterium.\nStatement II is correct: NCERT describes cheese as one of the oldest food items in which microbes were used.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (microbe) with List-II (product obtained commercially):\nList-I\nA. Aspergillus niger\nB. Acetobacter aceti\nC. Clostridium butylicum\nD. Saccharomyces cerevisiae\nList-II\n(i) Ethanol\n(ii) Butyric acid\n(iii) Citric acid\n(iv) Acetic acid","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Aspergillus niger, a fungus, gives citric acid (iii).\nB. Acetobacter aceti, a bacterium, gives acetic acid (iv).\nC. Clostridium butylicum, a bacterium, gives butyric acid (ii).\nD. Saccharomyces cerevisiae, a yeast, is used for commercial ethanol (i).\nOption (2) wrongly swaps the producers of citric acid and acetic acid.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the main sources of biofertilisers are:","image":"","options":["Bacteria, fungi and cyanobacteria","Bacteria, viruses and protozoa","Fungi, viroids and green algae","Cyanobacteria, prions and yeasts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Biofertilisers are organisms that enrich the nutrient quality of the soil.\nNCERT names three main sources: bacteria (e.g., Rhizobium, Azotobacter), fungi (e.g., Glomus in mycorrhiza) and cyanobacteria (e.g., Anabaena, Nostoc).\nViruses, protozoa, viroids and prions do not enrich soil nutrients, so the other options are wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about biological control:\n(i) Spores of Bacillus thuringiensis eaten by caterpillars release a toxin in the larval gut that kills them, while other insects remain unharmed.\n(ii) Nucleopolyhedroviruses are broad-spectrum insecticides, so they are preferred when beneficial insects must be conserved.\n(iii) Trichoderma species used against plant pathogens are free-living fungi common in root ecosystems.\n(iv) The organic farmer aims to eradicate pests completely so that predatory insects are protected.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i) and (iii) only","(i), (ii) and (iii) only","(iii) and (iv) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the Bt toxin acts in the larval gut and kills caterpillars but spares other insects.\n(ii) Incorrect: baculoviruses (genus Nucleopolyhedrovirus) are species-specific and narrow-spectrum. That narrow spectrum is why they suit IPM programmes that conserve beneficial insects.\n(iii) Correct: Trichoderma species are free-living fungi of root ecosystems and control several plant pathogens.\n(iv) Incorrect: the organic farmer considers eradicating pests undesirable. Predatory and parasitic insects depend on pests as food or hosts.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Bacteriophage\n(b) Adenovirus\n(c) Tobacco Mosaic Virus\n(d) Prion\nList-II\n(i) Rod-shaped virus infecting a plant\n(ii) Virus that infects bacteria\n(iii) Proteinaceous infectious agent\n(iv) Virus causing respiratory infections\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) A bacteriophage is a virus that infects bacteria, so (ii).\n(b) Adenovirus causes respiratory infections, so (iv).\n(c) TMV is a rod-shaped virus infecting tobacco, so (i).\n(d) A prion is a proteinaceous infectious agent, so (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which microbe is used for the commercial production of citric acid?","image":"","options":["Acetobacter aceti","Aspergillus niger","Clostridium butylicum","Lactobacillus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists Aspergillus niger, a fungus, as the producer of citric acid.\nAcetobacter aceti makes acetic acid.\nClostridium butylicum makes butyric acid.\nLactobacillus makes lactic acid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The technology of biogas (gobar gas) production in India was developed mainly through the efforts of which two organisations?","image":"","options":["Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR) and National Dairy Research Institute (NDRI)","Council of Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR) and National Botanical Research Institute (NBRI)","Indian Agricultural Research Institute (IARI) and Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC)","Indian Agricultural Research Institute (IARI) and Indian Council of Medical Research (ICMR)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT credits biogas technology in India mainly to the Indian Agricultural Research Institute (IARI) and the Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC).\nOption (4) is tempting because it includes IARI. However, its partner, ICMR, deals with medical research, not rural energy.\nOptions (1) and (2) name organisations that are not linked to biogas development in the chapter.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Statins, produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus, lower blood cholesterol by competitively inhibiting the enzyme responsible for cholesterol synthesis.\nStatement II: Streptokinase, produced by the fungus Trichoderma polysporum, is used as an immunosuppressive agent in organ-transplant patients.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: statins come from Monascus purpureus and act as competitive inhibitors of the cholesterol-synthesising enzyme.\nStatement II is incorrect: streptokinase comes from the bacterium Streptococcus. It is a 'clot buster' given after myocardial infarction.\nThe immunosuppressant made by Trichoderma polysporum is cyclosporin A.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Primary treatment of sewage removes large and small particles by physical means, namely filtration and sedimentation.\nStatement II: The biological treatment of waste water in sewage treatment plants is carried out by heterotrophic microbes naturally present in the sewage.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Primary treatment is purely physical. Floating debris is removed by sequential filtration, and grit is removed by sedimentation.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that sewage is treated by heterotrophic microbes that are naturally present in the sewage. They are not externally introduced autotrophs.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Whisky, brandy and rum are produced by distillation of the fermented broth.\nStatement II: The yeast used in brewing is a different species from the baker's yeast used for bread-making.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that whisky, brandy and rum are obtained by distilling the fermented broth.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT says the same yeast used for bread-making, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, is used for brewing and is commonly called brewer's yeast.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following pairs is INCORRECTLY matched?","image":"","options":["Propionibacterium sharmanii – large holes in Swiss cheese due to heavy $CO_2$ production","Trichoderma – free-living fungus of root ecosystems used as a biocontrol agent of plant pathogens","Monascus purpureus – product that competitively inhibits the enzyme for cholesterol synthesis","Roquefort cheese – ripened by growing a specific bacterium that gives its flavour"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correctly matched. Propionibacterium sharmanii releases large amounts of $CO_2$, which forms the holes in Swiss cheese.\n(2) Correctly matched. Trichoderma species act against several plant pathogens.\n(3) Correctly matched. Statins from Monascus purpureus competitively inhibit cholesterol synthesis.\n(4) Incorrectly matched. Roquefort cheese is ripened by a specific fungus, not a bacterium.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"In the aeration tanks of a sewage treatment plant, 'flocs' are best described as:","image":"","options":["Masses of bacteria associated with fungal filaments forming mesh-like structures","Clumps of grit and small pebbles that settle at the bottom","Floating debris removed by sequential filtration","Colonies of methanogenic bacteria that produce biogas"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: flocs are masses of bacteria associated with fungal filaments that form mesh-like structures. They develop through the vigorous growth of aerobic microbes in aeration tanks.\nGrit and floating debris are removed during primary treatment, not in aeration tanks.\nMethanogens grow anaerobically in sludge digesters, not as aerobic flocs.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about microbial bioactive molecules is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Streptokinase used as a clot buster has been modified by genetic engineering.","Cyclosporin A is obtained from the fungus Trichoderma polysporum.","Statins lower blood cholesterol by breaking down cholesterol already deposited in blood vessels.","Statins are produced by the yeast Monascus purpureus."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT says streptokinase is modified by genetic engineering.\n(2) Correct: Trichoderma polysporum, a fungus, produces cyclosporin A.\n(3) Incorrect: statins do not break down deposited cholesterol. They lower cholesterol by competitively inhibiting the enzyme responsible for cholesterol synthesis.\n(4) Correct: Monascus purpureus, a yeast, produces statins.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which pest is controlled by the ladybird, the familiar beetle with red and black markings?","image":"","options":["Mosquitoes","Aphids","Butterfly caterpillars","Root-borne fungal pathogens"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT names two insect predators used in biological pest control: the ladybird and the dragonfly.\nThe ladybird is used to get rid of aphids.\nDragonflies, not ladybirds, are used against mosquitoes, so option (1) is the tempting wrong choice.\nButterfly caterpillars are controlled by Bacillus thuringiensis. Root-borne pathogens are controlled by Trichoderma.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The fungus Aspergillus niger is used to produce citric acid.\n(ii) Lipases added to detergents help remove oily stains.\n(iii) Bottled fruit juices are clarified using pectinases and lipases.\n(iv) The same yeast species used as brewer's yeast is used for commercial production of ethanol.\n(v) Lactobacillus, used for lactic acid production, is a fungus.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct.\n(ii) Correct.\n(iii) Incorrect: juices are clarified with pectinases and proteases.\n(iv) Correct: Saccharomyces cerevisiae is both the brewer's yeast and the commercial ethanol producer.\n(v) Incorrect: Lactobacillus is a bacterium.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a free-living bacterium that fixes atmospheric nitrogen in the soil and so acts as a biofertiliser?","image":"","options":["Rhizobium","Azotobacter","Glomus","Methanobacterium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT names Azospirillum and Azotobacter as bacteria that fix atmospheric nitrogen while living freely in the soil.\nRhizobium also fixes nitrogen, but only symbiotically, inside the root nodules of legumes. So it is not free-living.\nGlomus is a fungus that forms mycorrhiza and supplies phosphorus to plants.\nMethanobacterium is a methanogen that produces biogas.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Penicillium notatum\nB. Ernest Chain and Howard Florey\nC. Staphylococci\nD. World War II\nList-II\n(i) Bacteria whose growth was inhibited on Fleming's culture plate\n(ii) Extensive use of penicillin to treat wounded American soldiers\n(iii) Mould after which penicillin was named\n(iv) Established the full potential of penicillin as an effective antibiotic","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Penicillin was named after the mould Penicillium notatum.\nB-(iv): Chain and Florey later established penicillin's full potential as an effective antibiotic.\nC-(i): Fleming saw that Staphylococci could not grow around the mould.\nD-(ii): Penicillin was extensively used for wounded American soldiers in World War II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following pairs of microbe and the organic acid it is used to produce commercially is INCORRECTLY matched?","image":"","options":["Aspergillus niger – Citric acid","Acetobacter aceti – Acetic acid","Clostridium butylicum – Lactic acid","Lactobacillus – Lactic acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists these industrial acid producers:\n(1) Aspergillus niger, a fungus, makes citric acid. Correct.\n(2) Acetobacter aceti, a bacterium, makes acetic acid. Correct.\n(3) Clostridium butylicum, a bacterium, makes butyric acid, not lactic acid. This pair is wrong.\n(4) Lactobacillus makes lactic acid. Correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following characteristic features are mainly due to carbon dioxide released by microbes?\n(i) Puffed-up appearance of dosa/idli dough\n(ii) Rising of bread dough\n(iii) Large holes in Swiss cheese\n(iv) Particular flavour of Roquefort cheese\n(v) Coagulation of milk proteins during curd formation","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: the dough puffs up due to $CO_2$ released by bacterial fermentation.\n(ii) Yes: yeast releases $CO_2$ that makes bread dough rise.\n(iii) Yes: $CO_2$ from Propionibacterium sharmanii forms the holes.\n(iv) No: the flavour comes from fungal ripening.\n(v) No: acids produced by lactic acid bacteria coagulate the milk proteins.\nSo 3 features are due to $CO_2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The 'clot buster' enzyme streptokinase is produced by which microbe?","image":"","options":["Staphylococcus","Streptococcus","Streptomyces","Trichoderma polysporum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that streptokinase is produced by the bacterium Streptococcus.\nIt is then modified by genetic engineering before it is used as a clot buster.\nStaphylococcus is the bacterium Fleming was working on when Penicillin was discovered, so it is a tempting but wrong choice.\nTrichoderma polysporum is the fungus that produces cyclosporin A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"In a sewage treatment plant, the mechanical agitation and air pumping in the aeration tanks fail for several days, while all other units keep working. Which outcome is most likely?","image":"","options":["BOD falls faster because anaerobic bacteria take over and convert the organic matter to biogas inside the aeration tank","Grit and pebbles start accumulating in the aeration tank instead of the primary tank","Useful aerobic flocs grow poorly, so much organic matter stays in the effluent and its BOD remains high","The amount of primary sludge formed decreases sharply"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Agitation and air supply allow aerobic microbes to grow vigorously into flocs. Without them, flocs do not develop properly.\nThe organic matter is therefore not consumed, and the effluent's BOD stays high.\nOption (1) is wrong because anaerobic biogas production takes place in separate sludge digesters, not in aeration tanks.\nOptions (2) and (4) are wrong because grit removal and primary sludge formation happen earlier, in primary treatment, which is unaffected.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (microbe) with List-II (acid commercially produced):\nList-I\n(A) Aspergillus niger\n(B) Acetobacter aceti\n(C) Clostridium butylicum\n(D) Lactobacillus\nList-II\n(I) Butyric acid\n(II) Lactic acid\n(III) Citric acid\n(IV) Acetic acid","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: Aspergillus niger is a fungus that produces citric acid, so A-III.\nB: Acetobacter aceti is a bacterium that produces acetic acid, so B-IV.\nC: Clostridium butylicum is a bacterium that produces butyric acid, so C-I.\nD: Lactobacillus is a bacterium that produces lactic acid, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Butyric acid is produced commercially using:","image":"","options":["Clostridium butylicum, a bacterium","Aspergillus niger, a fungus","Saccharomyces cerevisiae, a yeast","Acetobacter aceti, a fungus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, Clostridium butylicum, a bacterium, produces butyric acid.\nAspergillus niger produces citric acid.\nSaccharomyces cerevisiae produces ethanol.\nAcetobacter aceti produces acetic acid, and it is a bacterium, not a fungus.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (biofertiliser) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\n(a) Rhizobium\n(b) Azospirillum\n(c) Glomus\n(d) Anabaena\nList-II\n(i) Free-living nitrogen-fixing bacterium in soil\n(ii) Autotrophic nitrogen fixer important in paddy fields\n(iii) Symbiotic nitrogen fixer in legume root nodules\n(iv) Fungus forming mycorrhiza","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Rhizobium forms root nodules on legumes and fixes nitrogen symbiotically, so it matches (iii).\n(b) Azospirillum fixes nitrogen while free-living in soil, so it matches (i).\n(c) Glomus is a fungus that forms mycorrhiza, so it matches (iv).\n(d) Anabaena is an autotrophic cyanobacterium that fixes nitrogen and serves as a biofertiliser in paddy fields, so it matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"A flow diagram of a sewage treatment plant shows the following:\nTank X receives primary effluent, which is agitated while air is pumped in.\nThe liquid then goes to tank Y, where masses of microbes settle as sediment Z.\nA small part of Z is returned to tank X, and the rest is sent to tank W.\nWhich statement about this set-up is correct?","image":"","options":["Z is the primary sludge formed by sedimentation of grit and debris","In W, anaerobic bacteria digest the bacteria and fungi of the sludge, producing methane, hydrogen sulphide and carbon dioxide","The BOD of the effluent increases while it is in tank X","The flocs that form in tank X consist mainly of anaerobic bacteria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"X is the aeration tank. Y is the settling tank, Z is activated sludge and W is the anaerobic sludge digester.\n(1) Wrong: primary sludge forms in primary treatment. Z is activated sludge, and part of it is returned to X as inoculum.\n(2) Correct: in the digester, anaerobic bacteria digest the sludge microbes and produce biogas (CH4, H2S, CO2).\n(3) Wrong: aerobic microbes consume organic matter in X, so BOD decreases.\n(4) Wrong: the flocs are aerobic bacteria associated with fungal filaments.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the organic farming approach to pest control is incorrect?","image":"","options":["It holds that greater biodiversity in a landscape makes it more sustainable","It aims to keep pests at manageable levels through natural checks and balances","It aims at complete eradication of pests from the field","It requires knowing the life cycles, feeding patterns and preferred habitats of pests and predators"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: a key belief of the organic farmer is that biodiversity furthers health and sustainability.\n(2) Correct: pests are kept at manageable levels by checks and balances within the ecosystem.\n(3) Incorrect: eradicating pests is undesirable, because beneficial predatory and parasitic insects depend on them as food or hosts.\n(4) Correct: familiarity with these life forms helps develop suitable biocontrol.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The puffed-up appearance of the dough used for making idli and dosa is due to the production of $CO_2$ gas.\nReason (R): Idli and dosa dough is fermented using baker's yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The dough puffs up because $CO_2$ is released during fermentation.\nR is false. NCERT says idli and dosa dough is fermented by bacteria. Baker's yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) is the microbe used to ferment bread dough.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In NCERT figures, the flagella of a rod-shaped bacterium are shown at a much higher magnification (about 50,000X) than whole rod-shaped and spherical bacteria (about 1500X).\nStatement II: Viruses such as TMV and adenovirus are shown magnified about 1,00,000 to 1,50,000X because they are much smaller than bacteria.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Figure 8.1 shows rod-shaped and spherical bacteria at 1500X, and the flagellated bacterium at 50,000X.\nStatement II is correct. Figure 8.2 shows viruses at about 1,00,000 to 1,50,000X, since viruses are far smaller than bacteria.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Alexander Fleming\n(B) Ernest Chain and Howard Florey\n(C) Penicillium notatum\n(D) World War II\nList-II\n(i) Established the full potential of penicillin as an effective antibiotic\n(ii) Mould that produces penicillin\n(iii) Extensive use of penicillin for wounded soldiers\n(iv) Chance discovery of penicillin\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iv): Fleming discovered penicillin by chance.\nB-(i): Chain and Florey later established its full potential as an effective antibiotic.\nC-(ii): Penicillium notatum is the mould that produces penicillin.\nD-(iii): Penicillin was used extensively for wounded American soldiers in World War II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Waste water with a higher BOD has a greater polluting potential.\nReason (R): The BOD test directly measures the mass of organic matter present in one litre of water.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that the greater the BOD of waste water, the more is its polluting potential.\nR is false: the BOD test measures the rate of oxygen uptake by micro-organisms in a water sample. It is therefore only an indirect measure of organic matter. BOD itself is the oxygen that would be consumed if all organic matter in one litre were oxidised by bacteria.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which option correctly describes the metabolic process by which brewer's yeast forms ethanol from sugars in the fermenting broth?","image":"","options":["Aerobic respiration, in which pyruvic acid is completely oxidised in the Krebs cycle to release ethanol","Lactic acid fermentation, in which pyruvic acid is reduced to lactic acid that is then converted to ethanol","Anaerobic fermentation, in which pyruvic acid formed by glycolysis is converted to ethanol and $CO_2$, with NADH oxidised to $NAD^+$","Glycolysis alone, which directly yields ethanol without forming pyruvic acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Glucose is first broken down to pyruvic acid by glycolysis.\nUnder anaerobic conditions, yeast decarboxylates pyruvic acid, releasing $CO_2$, and reduces the product to ethanol.\nThis step reoxidises NADH to $NAD^+$, so glycolysis can continue.\nAerobic Krebs cycle oxidation yields $CO_2$ and water, not ethanol.\nLactic acid fermentation is carried out by bacteria such as LAB, not by yeast.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about penicillin is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["It was the first antibiotic to be discovered.","Its discovery was a chance observation on an unwashed culture plate.","It was extensively used to treat wounded American soldiers in World War II.","It was purified from the bacterium Staphylococcus, which kills other microbes."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: penicillin was the first antibiotic discovered.\n(2) Correct: it was a chance discovery on an unwashed plate.\n(3) Correct: it was used extensively for wounded American soldiers in World War II.\n(4) Incorrect: penicillin comes from the mould Penicillium notatum. Staphylococcus was the bacterium whose growth it inhibited.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Dried spores of Bacillus thuringiensis sprayed on plants kill butterfly caterpillars that eat them, but leave other insects unharmed.\nStatement II: Trichoderma species are free-living fungi, common in root ecosystems, that act as biocontrol agents against several plant pathogens.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: the caterpillars eat the Bt spores and the toxin is released in their gut. This kills the caterpillars while other insects are spared.\nStatement II is correct: NCERT describes Trichoderma as free-living fungi in root ecosystems and effective biocontrol agents of plant pathogens.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (food product) with List-II (microbe involved):\nList-I\n(a) Curd\n(b) Bread dough\n(c) Large holes in Swiss cheese\n(d) Ripening of Roquefort cheese\nList-II\n(i) Saccharomyces cerevisiae\n(ii) Propionibacterium sharmanii\n(iii) Lactobacillus\n(iv) A specific fungus","image":"","options":["a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-ii, b-i, c-iii, d-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Curd: made by Lactobacillus (LAB), so a-iii.\n(b) Bread dough: fermented by baker's yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, so b-i.\n(c) Swiss cheese holes: caused by large amounts of $CO_2$ from Propionibacterium sharmanii, so c-ii.\n(d) Roquefort cheese: ripened by growing a specific fungus on it, so d-iv.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The dung (gobar) of cattle is used as the starting material for generating biogas.\nReason (R): The rumen of cattle contains methanogenic bacteria that help break down the cellulose in their food, so these bacteria are abundant in the dung.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Cattle dung is fed as a slurry into biogas plants, and the gas produced is called gobar gas.\nR is true. Methanogens such as Methanobacterium live in the cattle rumen and help digest cellulose, which aids cattle nutrition. As a result, the dung is rich in these bacteria.\nR explains A. Dung is useful for biogas because it carries methanogens that produce methane anaerobically.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"In which form is Bacillus thuringiensis made available to farmers for controlling butterfly caterpillars?","image":"","options":["As live adult insects released into the field","As dried spores in sachets, mixed with water and sprayed on plants","As a fungal slurry added to the soil around the roots","As a gas fumigated into closed storage rooms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Bt is sold in sachets as dried spores.\nThe spores are mixed with water and sprayed onto vulnerable plants such as brassicas and fruit trees.\nInsect larvae eat the sprayed leaves and are killed.\nReleasing live insects is the method used for predators such as ladybirds, not for a bacterium, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Microbe X is the only fungus among NCERT's examples of commercial organic acid producers. Microbe Y is a bacterium that produces the acid found in vinegar. Identify X, Y and the acid made by X.","image":"","options":["X = Saccharomyces cerevisiae, Y = Lactobacillus; X makes ethanol","X = Aspergillus niger, Y = Acetobacter aceti; X makes citric acid","X = Aspergillus niger, Y = Clostridium butylicum; X makes acetic acid","X = Penicillium notatum, Y = Acetobacter aceti; X makes citric acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The organic acid producers listed in NCERT are A. niger, A. aceti, C. butylicum and Lactobacillus. Only Aspergillus niger is a fungus, and it makes citric acid. So X = A. niger.\nVinegar contains acetic acid, which is produced by the bacterium Acetobacter aceti. So Y = A. aceti.\nOption (1) fails because ethanol is not an organic acid.\nOption (4) fails because Penicillium notatum is the source of penicillin, not citric acid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Wine and beer are produced without distillation, whereas whisky, brandy and rum are obtained by distilling the fermented broth.\nStatement II: The brewer's yeast used for alcoholic beverages belongs to a different species from the baker's yeast used for bread.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT says wine and beer are made without distillation, while whisky, brandy and rum need distillation.\nStatement II is incorrect: brewer's yeast is the same species as baker's yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Streptokinase used as a clot buster is produced by a fungus.\n(ii) Statins lower blood cholesterol by competitively inhibiting the enzyme responsible for cholesterol synthesis.\n(iii) Monascus purpureus, the source of statins, is a bacterium.\n(iv) The streptokinase used clinically has been modified by genetic engineering.\n(v) Cyclosporin A is obtained from a fungus.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: streptokinase is produced by the bacterium Streptococcus.\n(ii) Correct: statins compete with the substrate for the enzyme that synthesises cholesterol.\n(iii) Incorrect: Monascus purpureus is a yeast, which is a fungus, not a bacterium.\n(iv) Correct: streptokinase is modified by genetic engineering before use as a clot buster.\n(v) Correct: cyclosporin A comes from the fungus Trichoderma polysporum.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The dough used for making idli and dosa looks puffed up after it is left to ferment. What causes this?","image":"","options":["Carbon dioxide released during bacterial fermentation","Oxygen released by lactic acid bacteria","Methane released by methanogens","Hydrogen sulphide released by anaerobic bacteria"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Idli and dosa dough is fermented by bacteria.\nDuring fermentation the bacteria release $CO_2$ gas, which gets trapped and makes the dough puff up.\nMethane and hydrogen sulphide are gases of biogas and sludge digesters, not of dough.\nBacteria do not release oxygen during fermentation, so option (2) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"A farmer grows a non-leguminous crop on soil that is low in available phosphorus, somewhat saline and prone to dry spells. Root infections have also been a problem. Based on NCERT, which single biofertiliser would address the largest number of these problems?","image":"","options":["Rhizobium culture","Azotobacter culture","Anabaena (blue-green alga) inoculum","Glomus (mycorrhizal fungus) inoculum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The problems are low phosphorus, salinity, drought and root-borne pathogens.\nGlomus mycorrhiza passes phosphorus to the plant and gives resistance to root-borne pathogens, tolerance to salinity and drought, and better growth. It matches all four problems.\nRhizobium forms nodules only in legumes and supplies nitrogen, so it does not suit this crop.\nAzotobacter and Anabaena mainly add nitrogen (Anabaena chiefly in paddy fields), which is not the stated need.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about sewage treatment is correct?","image":"","options":["Primary treatment is a biological process carried out mainly by aerobic microbes","The sediment of bacterial flocs in the settling tank is called activated sludge, and a small part of it is returned to the aeration tank as inoculum","The BOD of the primary effluent rises steadily during vigorous aeration in the aeration tank","The major part of the activated sludge is released directly into rivers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: primary treatment is physical. Particles are removed by filtration and sedimentation.\n(2) Correct: the flocs that settle form activated sludge, and a small part of it is pumped back to serve as inoculum.\n(3) Incorrect: microbes in the aeration tank consume the organic matter, so BOD falls.\n(4) Incorrect: the major part of the sludge goes to anaerobic sludge digesters.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The secondary effluent from a sewage treatment plant has a much lower BOD than untreated sewage, because aerobic microbes in the flocs consume the major part of the organic matter.\nStatement II: The BOD test directly measures the mass of organic matter present in one litre of a water sample.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. During secondary treatment, flocs oxidise most of the organic matter, so the BOD falls sharply.\nStatement II is incorrect. The BOD test measures the rate of oxygen uptake by microbes. It is therefore only an indirect measure of organic matter, not a direct measure of its mass.\nA higher BOD means greater polluting potential.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following can fix atmospheric nitrogen?\nRhizobium, Azotobacter, Glomus, Azospirillum, Anabaena, Trichoderma, Nostoc","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rhizobium: fixes nitrogen symbiotically in legume root nodules.\nAzotobacter and Azospirillum: free-living nitrogen fixers in soil.\nAnabaena and Nostoc: nitrogen-fixing cyanobacteria.\nGlomus is a mycorrhizal fungus that supplies phosphorus, not nitrogen. Trichoderma is a fungus used as a biocontrol agent.\nTotal nitrogen fixers = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about a gobar gas plant is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["It has a concrete tank about 10–15 feet deep into which a slurry of dung is fed","Spent slurry is removed through a separate outlet and can be used as fertiliser","The gas produced is supplied through a pipe and used for cooking and lighting","Such plants are built mostly in cities because large volumes of urban sewage are available"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT describes a 10–15 ft deep concrete tank fed with dung slurry.\n(2) Correct: spent slurry leaves through another outlet and may be used as fertiliser.\n(3) Correct: a pipe from the gas outlet supplies houses for cooking and lighting.\n(4) Incorrect: these plants are built more often in rural areas, where large quantities of cattle dung are available.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"The adenovirus shown in NCERT Figure 8.2 is known to cause:","image":"","options":["Mosaic disease of tobacco leaves","Respiratory infections","Lysis of bacterial cells","Coagulation of milk proteins"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT's figure caption describes adenovirus as a virus that causes respiratory infections.\nOption (1) fits Tobacco Mosaic Virus (TMV), the rod-shaped virus in the same figure.\nOption (3) fits a bacteriophage.\nOption (4) is the work of lactic acid bacteria, not a virus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Primary treatment of sewage is also called biological treatment.\nReason (R): Primary treatment involves physical removal of large and small particles by filtration and sedimentation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false: it is secondary treatment that is called biological treatment, because aerobic microbes in aeration tanks consume the organic matter.\nR is true: primary treatment removes floating debris by sequential filtration and grit by sedimentation, which are purely physical steps.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Curd is nutritionally better than the milk from which it is made.\nReason (R): Lactic acid bacteria growing in milk increase its vitamin $B_{12}$ content.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that converting milk to curd improves its nutritional quality.\nR is true: LAB such as Lactobacillus increase vitamin $B_{12}$ while they grow in milk.\nR explains A, because the nutritional improvement comes from this rise in vitamin $B_{12}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Cyclosporin A, a bioactive molecule obtained from a fungus, is medically used for:","image":"","options":["lowering blood cholesterol levels","dissolving clots in blood vessels after a heart attack","suppressing the immune response in organ-transplant patients","killing Staphylococci in infected wounds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cyclosporin A is an immunosuppressive agent. It is given to organ-transplant patients to dampen the immune response so that the transplanted organ is not rejected.\nLowering cholesterol is the role of statins.\nDissolving clots is the role of streptokinase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Milk to which a curd starter has been added sets into curd much more slowly if it is kept in a refrigerator instead of at room temperature in summer.\nStatement II: Lactic acid bacteria multiply and convert milk into curd at suitable temperatures.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct, and Statement II explains Statement I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct: NCERT states that the LAB in the starter multiply at suitable temperatures and convert milk to curd.\nStatement I is correct: refrigeration temperature is far below the suitable range, so LAB multiplication and acid production slow down greatly.\nWith slower acid production, coagulation of milk proteins is delayed, so Statement II explains Statement I.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about biofertilisers are correct?\n(i) Plants with mycorrhiza show resistance to root-borne pathogens and tolerance to salinity and drought.\n(ii) Rhizobium fixes atmospheric nitrogen while living freely in the soil, without any host plant.\n(iii) Cyanobacteria such as Nostoc and Oscillatoria fix nitrogen and also add organic matter to the soil.\n(iv) The main sources of biofertilisers are bacteria, fungi and cyanobacteria.\n(v) Cyanobacteria are heterotrophic microbes found only in aquatic habitats.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT lists these benefits of mycorrhizal plants, along with better overall growth.\n(ii) Incorrect: Rhizobium fixes nitrogen symbiotically in the root nodules of legumes. Azospirillum and Azotobacter are the free-living fixers.\n(iii) Correct: blue-green algae fix nitrogen and add organic matter, which increases soil fertility.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT names bacteria, fungi and cyanobacteria as the main sources.\n(v) Incorrect: cyanobacteria are autotrophic and occur in both aquatic and terrestrial environments.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Sewage is treated in STPs mainly by heterotrophic microbes that are naturally present in the sewage.\nStatement II: The Ganga Action Plan and Yamuna Action Plan were initiated by the Ministry of Health to vaccinate people against water-borne diseases.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT states that waste water is treated by the heterotrophic microbes naturally present in sewage.\nStatement II is incorrect on two counts.\nFirst, the plans were initiated by the Ministry of Environment and Forests, not the Ministry of Health.\nSecond, their aim is to build many STPs so that only treated sewage is discharged into these rivers, not to vaccinate people.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Methanobacterium\n(B) Rumen\n(C) Floating cover\n(D) Spent slurry\nList-II\n(I) Used as fertiliser\n(II) Rises as gas collects in the digester\n(III) Part of cattle stomach where cellulose is broken down\n(IV) Methane-producing bacterium\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-IV: Methanobacterium is a methanogen.\nB-III: the rumen is part of the cattle stomach where methanogens help digest cellulose.\nC-II: the floating cover rises as biogas accumulates.\nD-I: spent slurry is used as fertiliser.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about lactic acid bacteria (LAB) is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The acids produced by LAB coagulate and partially digest milk proteins.","A small amount of curd used as starter contains millions of LAB.","Conversion of milk to curd by LAB lowers the nutritional quality of milk.","In the human stomach, LAB help in checking disease-causing microbes."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: LAB produce acids that coagulate and partially digest milk proteins.\n(2) Correct: a small amount of curd added as inoculum contains millions of LAB.\n(3) Incorrect: curd formation improves nutritional quality, because vitamin $B_{12}$ increases.\n(4) Correct: in the stomach, LAB play a beneficial role in checking pathogens.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Primary sludge\n(B) Primary effluent\n(C) Activated sludge\n(D) Inoculum\nList-II\n(i) Small portion of sediment pumped back into the aeration tank\n(ii) Supernatant of the primary settling tank\n(iii) All solids that settle during primary treatment\n(iv) Sediment of bacterial flocs in the settling tank after aeration","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A–(iii): All the solids that settle in primary treatment form the primary sludge.\nB–(ii): The supernatant left above them is the primary effluent.\nC–(iv): After the BOD is reduced, the flocs settle in a settling tank, and this sediment is the activated sludge.\nD–(i): A small part of the activated sludge is returned to the aeration tank to act as the inoculum.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"A farmer protects his crop against caterpillars in two ways. In field X, he sprays Bacillus thuringiensis spores. In field Y, he grows Bt-cotton. Which statement correctly distinguishes the two methods?","image":"","options":["In field X the toxin is produced by the plant itself, while in field Y it comes from the bacterium sprayed on the leaves","In field Y the Bt toxin gene has been introduced into the plant by genetic engineering, so the plant itself resists insect pests, while in field X the bacterium must be applied externally","Both fields rely on Trichoderma present in the root ecosystem to kill the larvae","Field Y uses a baculovirus genome inserted into cotton, while field X uses chemical insecticide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Field X: dried Bt spores are mixed with water and sprayed on the plants. Larvae eat them and the toxin released in their gut kills them, so the bacterium is applied from outside.\nField Y: genetic engineering has introduced Bt toxin genes into the cotton plant, which therefore resists insect attack by itself.\nOption (1) reverses the two methods.\nOptions (3) and (4) name the wrong agents.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-BIO-MICROBES-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Microbes in Human Welfare","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Microbes in Human Welfare","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about fermented beverages:\n(i) The yeast used to produce alcoholic beverages is a different species from baker's yeast.\n(ii) Brewer's yeast ferments malted cereals and fruit juices to produce ethanol.\n(iii) Whisky and rum are obtained by distilling the fermented broth.\n(iv) Wine and brandy are both produced without distillation.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: the same species, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, is used for bread-making and is also called brewer's yeast.\n(ii) Correct: brewer's yeast ferments malted cereals and fruit juices to give ethanol.\n(iii) Correct: whisky, brandy and rum are made by distilling the fermented broth.\n(iv) Incorrect: wine is undistilled, but brandy is distilled.\nSo two statements, (ii) and (iii), are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Biology, Ch 8 · Microbes in Human Welfare","difficulty":"medium"}]});
